export const PRESETS = [{"id":"W-01","title":"Hello, dialect","group":"Basics","about":"define a dialect, trace a program into a graph, simplify it with a pass of rewrite rules","tries":["Add a rule that folds x - x into 0","Why did const_right fire before fold?"],"text":"/** W-01 Hello, dialect\n *  group  Basics\n *  about  define a dialect, trace a program into a graph, simplify it with a pass of rewrite rules\n *  try    Add a rule that folds x - x into 0 | Why did const_right fire before fold?\n */\n// A dialect is a set of ops. Each op says how to infer its type, how to evaluate it (so any graph made of\n// it can be interpreted, and every rewrite checked), and how to print. Methods let traced code read\n// like ordinary math: x.mul(3).add(y).\nconst Arith = k.dialect(\"arith\")\n  .op(\"const\", { attrs: { value: 0 }, traits: [\"pure\", \"const\"], infer: () => k.scalar(), eval: ({ value }) => value, print: \"{value}\" })\n  .op(\"add\", { args: [\"a\", \"b\"], traits: [\"pure\", \"commutative\"], infer: ({ a }) => a.type, eval: ({ a, b }) => a + b, print: \"{a} + {b}\" })\n  .op(\"sub\", { args: [\"a\", \"b\"], traits: [\"pure\"], infer: ({ a }) => a.type, eval: ({ a, b }) => a - b, print: \"{a} - {b}\" })\n  .op(\"mul\", { args: [\"a\", \"b\"], traits: [\"pure\", \"commutative\"], infer: ({ a }) => a.type, eval: ({ a, b }) => a * b, print: \"{a} * {b}\" })\n  .lifts((v) => Arith.const({ value: v }))        // bare numbers in traced code become consts\n  .methods({ add: (a, b) => Arith.add(a, b), sub: (a, b) => Arith.sub(a, b), mul: (a, b) => Arith.mul(a, b) });\n\n// Rules match a pattern and return a replacement. Three ways to write the match:\nconst constRight = k.rule(\"const_right\")              // 1. a pattern with a predicate\n  .match(P.trait(\"commutative\").as(\"n\").where(({ n }) => n.arg(0).isConst() && !n.arg(1).isConst()))\n  .rewrite(({ n }) => n.with({ args: [n.arg(1), n.arg(0)] }));\nconst fold = k.rule(\"fold\")                            // 2. any pure op whose args are all constants\n  .match(P.trait(\"pure\").as(\"n\").where(({ n }) => n.args.length > 0 && n.args.every((a) => a.isConst())))\n  .rewrite(({ n }) => Arith.const({ value: n.fold() }));\nconst mulOne = k.rule(\"mul_one\").from(({ x }) => x.mul(1)).to(({ x }) => x);     // 3. by example\nconst addZero = k.rule(\"add_zero\").from(({ x }) => x.add(0)).to(({ x }) => x);\nconst mulZero = k.rule(\"mul_zero\").from(({ x }) => x.mul(0)).to(() => Arith.const({ value: 0 }));\nconst twice = k.rule(\"x*2 → x+x\").from(({ x }) => x.mul(2)).to(({ x }) => x.add(x));\n\n// A pass runs its rules to a fixpoint; a pipeline alternates IR checkpoints and passes.\nconst simplify = k.pass(\"simplify\").short(\"s\").rules(constRight, fold, mulOne, addZero, mulZero, twice)\n  .describe(\"canonicalize constants to the right, fold, and apply algebraic identities\");\nconst pipe = k.pipeline(\"calc\")\n  .ir(\"arith\", { dialects: [Arith] })\n  .pass(simplify)\n  .ir(\"simple\", { dialects: [Arith], strict: true })\n  .build();\n\nproject(\"calc\")\n  .pipeline(pipe)\n  .program(({ x, y }) => ({ out: x.mul(Arith.add(3, -2)).add(0).add(y.mul(2)).add(Arith.mul(0, y)) }), { x: k.scalar(), y: k.scalar() })\n  .data({ x: 3, y: 4 })\n  .check(\"folds to x + (y + y): two inputs, two adds\", ({ run }) => run.graph.size === 4 || `expected 4 nodes, got ${run.graph.size}`)\n  .check(\"same value as the source\", ({ outputs }) => outputs.out === 11 || `got ${outputs.out}`);\n","notes":"# Hello, dialect\n\nThe smallest complete compiler: a dialect, a program, a pass of rewrite rules, and a pipeline with checkpoints. Everything else in the workbench is this pattern at a larger scale.\n\n> **Try it** — open the [simplified IR](lab:ir?name=simple), then step through the rewrites in [Trace](lab:view?name=trace). Each step shows its diff. Then open the [Graph](lab:graph?name=arith) of the source.\n\n## The pieces\n\n**A dialect** is a set of operations. Each op declares:\n\n- `args` and `attrs`: its operands and constants;\n- `infer`: its result type;\n- `eval`: what it computes. Any graph built from evaluable ops can be interpreted, so every rewrite can be checked;\n- `print`: how it shows up in the IR view;\n- `traits` such as `pure` (no side effects: it can be folded and deduplicated), `commutative` and `const`.\n\n**Tracing.** `k.trace(name, fn, inputTypes)` calls your JavaScript function with symbolic values. Every dialect call becomes a node, and the result is a dataflow graph (a DAG). `.lifts()` turns bare numbers into constants; `.methods()` lets you write `x.mul(3).add(y)`.\n\n**Rules** match a pattern and return a replacement. Three ways to write the match:\n\n| style | example | when |\n|---|---|---|\n| pattern + predicate | `P.trait(\"pure\").where(({n}) => …)` | general conditions |\n| typed pattern | `P.op(\"arith.mul\").args(P.any(), P.const(1))` | fixed shapes |\n| by example | `.from(({x}) => x.mul(1)).to(({x}) => x)` | algebraic identities |\n\n**A pass** runs its rules to a **fixpoint**: rewrite the first match, rescan, repeat until nothing matches. **A pipeline** alternates IR checkpoints and passes. A `strict` checkpoint fails the compile if an op from another dialect survives.\n\n## What happens to this program\n\n$$\\text{out} = x \\cdot (3 + (-2)) + 0 + y \\cdot 2 + 0 \\cdot y$$\n\nThe rewrites, in order (see [Trace](lab:view?name=trace)):\n\n1. `fold` turns $3 + (-2)$ into $1$.\n2. `mul_one` turns $x \\cdot 1$ into $x$.\n3. `add_zero` drops the first $+0$.\n4. `x*2 → x+x` turns $y \\cdot 2$ into $y + y$ (addition is cheaper than multiplication on many targets).\n5. `const_right` moves the constant in $0 \\cdot y$ to the right, which lets `mul_zero` match.\n6. `mul_zero` and `add_zero` finish.\n\nThe final graph has 4 nodes: $x + (y + y)$. The checks in [Code](lab:view?name=code) verify the size, and that the value is unchanged on $x = 3, y = 4$.\n\n## Why rule order and canonical forms matter\n\n`mul_zero` is written as `x.mul(0)`: it matches only when the constant is the **second** argument. Rather than writing every rule twice, `const_right` establishes a **canonical form** (constants on the right) and the other rules assume it. This is how production rewriters such as MLIR's canonicalization keep the rule count manageable.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Add a rule for $x - x \\to 0$ (you'll need a `sub` op). Does it fire on `x.sub(x)` after other rewrites?\n2. Write a rule that would loop forever (e.g. $a + b \\to b + a$ without a condition) and watch the `FixpointError`. How does `const_right` avoid it?\n3. Add a `strict: true` checkpoint *before* `simplify`. Why does it still pass?\n\n## Further reading\n\n- MLIR [Toy tutorial](https://mlir.llvm.org/docs/Tutorials/Toy/), chapters 2–3: dialects and pattern rewrites\n- Cooper & Torczon, *Engineering a Compiler*: the chapters on IR and value numbering\n"},{"id":"W-02","title":"Patterns, declines and guards","group":"Basics","about":"strength reduction, chained patterns, CSE in one rule, and rules that say no (and why)","tries":["Turn on Declined in the Trace view: why was y * 6 left alone?","Make distribute fire anyway"],"text":"/** W-02 Patterns, declines and guards\n *  group  Basics\n *  about  strength reduction, chained patterns, CSE in one rule, and rules that say no (and why)\n *  try    Turn on Declined in the Trace view: why was y * 6 left alone? | Make distribute fire anyway\n */\nconst I = k.dialect(\"int\")\n  .op(\"const\", { attrs: { value: 0 }, traits: [\"pure\", \"const\"], infer: () => k.scalar(\"i32\"), eval: ({ value }) => value, print: \"{value}\" })\n  .op(\"add\", { args: [\"a\", \"b\"], traits: [\"pure\", \"commutative\"], infer: ({ a }) => a.type, eval: ({ a, b }) => (a + b) | 0, print: \"{a} + {b}\" })\n  .op(\"mul\", { args: [\"a\", \"b\"], traits: [\"pure\", \"commutative\"], infer: ({ a }) => a.type, eval: ({ a, b }) => Math.imul(a, b), print: \"{a} * {b}\" })\n  .op(\"shl\", { args: [\"a\", \"b\"], traits: [\"pure\"], infer: ({ a }) => a.type, eval: ({ a, b }) => a << b, print: \"{a} << {b}\" })\n  .lifts((v) => I.const({ value: v }))\n  .methods({ add: (a, b) => I.add(a, b), mul: (a, b) => I.mul(a, b), shl: (a, b) => I.shl(a, b) });\n\nconst isPow2 = (c) => Number.isInteger(c) && c > 0 && (c & (c - 1)) === 0;\n\n// x * 2^k → x << k. A rule declines with skip(reason): the Trace shows the reason (turn on Declined).\nconst strength = k.rule(\"strength_reduce\")\n  .match(P.op(\"int.mul\").as(\"m\").where(({ m }) => m.arg(1).isConst()))\n  .rewrite(({ m, skip }) => {\n    const c = m.arg(1).value;\n    if (!isPow2(c)) skip(`${c} is not a power of two`);\n    return I.shl(m.arg(0), Math.log2(c));\n  });\n\n// (x << a) << b → x << (a + b). P.chain matches a producer → consumer pair; the inner node must have one use.\nconst shlShl = k.rule(\"shl_shl\")\n  .match(P.chain(P.op(\"int.shl\").as(\"inner\").where(({ inner }) => inner.arg(1).isConst()),\n                 P.op(\"int.shl\").as(\"outer\").where(({ outer }) => outer.arg(1).isConst())))\n  .rewrite(({ inner, outer }) => I.shl(inner.arg(0), inner.arg(1).value + outer.arg(1).value));\n\n// common subexpressions: a pure node whose structural key matches an earlier node's is that node\nconst cse = k.rule(\"cse\")\n  .match(P.trait(\"pure\").as(\"n\").where(({ n }) => !n.isConst()))\n  .rewrite(({ n, graph }) => graph.findBefore(n.id, (v) => v.id !== n.id && v.key() === n.key()) ?? undefined);\n\n// a * (b + c) → a*b + a*c, but only if the graph does not grow: a guard sees before and after and can veto\nconst distribute = k.rule(\"distribute\")\n  .from(({ a, b, c }) => a.mul(b.add(c))).to(({ a, b, c }) => a.mul(b).add(a.mul(c)))\n  .guard(({ before, after }) => after.size <= before.size || `would grow the graph from ${before.size} to ${after.size} nodes`);\n\nconst pipe = k.pipeline(\"int-opt\")\n  .ir(\"int\", { dialects: [I] })\n  .pass(k.pass(\"strength\").short(\"s\").rules(strength, shlShl))\n  .pass(k.pass(\"cleanup\").short(\"c\").rules(cse, distribute))\n  .ir(\"optimized\", { dialects: [I], strict: true })\n  .build();\n\nproject(\"int-opt\")\n  .pipeline(pipe)\n  .program(({ x, y, z }) => ({ out: x.mul(8).mul(4).add(y.mul(6)).add(x.mul(8).mul(4)).add(z.mul(y.add(3))) }), { x: k.scalar(\"i32\"), y: k.scalar(\"i32\"), z: k.scalar(\"i32\") })\n  .data({ x: 5, y: -7, z: 3 })\n  .check(\"x * 8 * 4 became one shift, computed once\", ({ run }) => run.graph.findAll(\"int.shl\").length === 1 || run.graph.print())\n  .check(\"same value\", ({ outputs, reference }) => outputs.out === reference.out || `got ${outputs.out}, want ${reference.out}`);\n","notes":"# Patterns, declines and guards\n\nReal rewrite rules are conditional: they apply only when it is legal and profitable. This project shows the three ways a rule says no, and why each one is visible in the Trace.\n\n> **Try it** — open [Trace](lab:view?name=trace) and turn on *Declined*. Every refusal is listed with its reason. Then compare the [source IR](lab:ir?name=int) with the [optimized IR](lab:ir?name=optimized).\n\n## The rules\n\n**Strength reduction.** Integer multiplication by a power of two is a left shift:\n\n$$x \\cdot 2^k = x \\ll k$$\n\n`strength_reduce` matches any multiply by a constant and calls `skip(\"6 is not a power of two\")` for the others. A *skip* is a visible decline: the Trace records the rule, the node and the reason.\n\n**Chained patterns.** `P.chain(inner, outer)` matches a producer feeding a consumer, where the producer has exactly one use. Two shifts merge:\n\n$$(x \\ll a) \\ll b = x \\ll (a + b)$$\n\nThe one-use condition matters: if the inner shift had other users, merging would duplicate work instead of removing it.\n\n**Common-subexpression elimination in one rule.** Every node has a structural *key* (a hash of its op, attributes and its operands' keys). `cse` replaces a pure node by an earlier node with the same key. The two copies of `x * 8 * 4` become one shift, computed once.\n\n**Guards.** `distribute` rewrites $a \\cdot (b + c) \\to ab + ac$, then a guard compares the graphs before and after and vetoes the rewrite if the graph grew. The Trace shows *would grow the graph from 13 to 14 nodes*. A guard sees both versions, so it can apply any cost criterion.\n\n## Three ways to say no\n\n| mechanism | when it runs | visible in Trace? | use for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `.where(…)` on the pattern | while matching | no (not a match) | structural conditions |\n| `skip(\"why\")` in the rewrite | after matching, before building | yes, with your reason | conditions you want explained |\n| `.guard(…)` | after building the replacement | yes, with the guard's reason | profitability checks on the result |\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Make `distribute` fire anyway by relaxing its guard, then add a `cse` step after it. Does the graph end up smaller than before?\n2. Strength-reduce `x * 6` into `(x << 2) + (x << 1)`. Is it a win? Write a guard that decides.\n3. Why does `cse` use `graph.findBefore` rather than searching the whole graph?\n\n## Further reading\n\n- Muchnick, *Advanced Compiler Design and Implementation*: strength reduction, value numbering\n- Willsey et al., [egg: *Fast and Extensible Equality Saturation*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.03082): when rule order stops mattering\n"},{"id":"W-03","title":"Lowering to a register VM","group":"Basics","about":"lower one dialect into another under a strict IR, then a whole-graph pass allocates registers and emits code","tries":["Turn reuse off in Knobs and compare the register count","Add a fused multiply-add instruction"],"text":"/** W-03 Lowering to a register VM\n *  group  Basics\n *  about  lower one dialect into another under a strict IR, then a whole-graph pass allocates registers and emits code\n *  try    Turn reuse off in Knobs and compare the register count | Add a fused multiply-add instruction\n */\nconst OPS = { add: (a, b) => a + b, sub: (a, b) => a - b, mul: (a, b) => a * b, neg: (a) => -a };\n\n// the source language\nconst A = k.dialect(\"arith\")\n  .op(\"const\", { attrs: { value: 0 }, traits: [\"pure\", \"const\"], infer: () => k.scalar(), eval: ({ value }) => value, print: \"{value}\" })\n  .op(\"add\", { args: [\"a\", \"b\"], traits: [\"pure\"], infer: ({ a }) => a.type, eval: ({ a, b }) => a + b, print: \"{a} + {b}\" })\n  .op(\"sub\", { args: [\"a\", \"b\"], traits: [\"pure\"], infer: ({ a }) => a.type, eval: ({ a, b }) => a - b, print: \"{a} - {b}\" })\n  .op(\"mul\", { args: [\"a\", \"b\"], traits: [\"pure\"], infer: ({ a }) => a.type, eval: ({ a, b }) => a * b, print: \"{a} * {b}\" })\n  .op(\"neg\", { args: [\"a\"], traits: [\"pure\"], infer: ({ a }) => a.type, eval: ({ a }) => -a, print: \"-{a}\" })\n  .lifts((v) => A.const({ value: v }))\n  .methods({ add: (a, b) => A.add(a, b), sub: (a, b) => A.sub(a, b), mul: (a, b) => A.mul(a, b), neg: (a) => A.neg(a) });\n\n// the target: three-address instructions, still a graph (so it still evaluates)\nconst Vm = k.dialect(\"vm\")\n  .op(\"imm\", { attrs: { value: 0 }, traits: [\"pure\", \"const\"], infer: () => k.scalar(), eval: ({ value }) => value, print: \"imm {value}\" })\n  .op(\"bin\", { args: [\"a\", \"b\"], attrs: { opcode: \"add\" }, traits: [\"pure\"], infer: ({ a }) => a.type, eval: ({ a, b, opcode }) => OPS[opcode](a, b), print: \"{opcode} {a}, {b}\" })\n  .op(\"un\", { args: [\"a\"], attrs: { opcode: \"neg\" }, traits: [\"pure\"], infer: ({ a }) => a.type, eval: ({ a, opcode }) => OPS[opcode](a), print: \"{opcode} {a}\" });\n\n// …and finally a linear program: one node holding the instruction list; its eval is a tiny interpreter\nfunction exec(code, names, ins) {\n  const env = Object.fromEntries(names.map((n, i) => [\"%\" + n, ins[i]])), R = [];\n  const val = (s) => (s.startsWith(\"%\") ? env[s] : R[+s.slice(1)]);\n  for (const line of code) {\n    let m;\n    if ((m = line.match(/^r(\\d+) = imm (\\S+)$/))) R[+m[1]] = +m[2];\n    else if ((m = line.match(/^r(\\d+) = (\\w+) (.+)$/))) R[+m[1]] = OPS[m[2]](...m[3].split(\", \").map(val));\n    else if ((m = line.match(/^ret (\\S+)$/))) return val(m[1]);\n  }\n}\nconst Asm = k.dialect(\"asm\").op(\"program\", {\n  args: { variadic: \"ins\" }, attrs: { code: [], regs: 0, names: [] }, infer: () => k.scalar(),\n  eval: ({ ins, code, names }) => exec(code, names, ins),\n  print: ({ regs, code }) => `program · ${code.length} instructions · ${regs} registers`,\n  body: ({ code }) => code.join(\"\\n\"),\n});\n\n// lowering: every arith op becomes a vm op. The strict \"vm\" checkpoint fails the compile if any op is left behind.\nconst lowerBin = k.rule(\"lower_binary\").match(P.oneOf(\"arith.add\", \"arith.sub\", \"arith.mul\").as(\"n\")).rewrite(({ n }) => n.lower(Vm.bin({ opcode: n.opName })));\nconst lowerNeg = k.rule(\"lower_neg\").match(P.op(\"arith.neg\").as(\"n\")).rewrite(({ n }) => n.lower(Vm.un({ opcode: \"neg\" })));\nconst lowerConst = k.rule(\"lower_const\").match(P.op(\"arith.const\").as(\"n\")).rewrite(({ n }) => n.lower(Vm.imm({ value: n.attrs.value })));\n\n// a whole-graph pass: linear-scan register allocation over the topological order, then emission\nconst REUSE = param.bool(\"reuse\", { default: true, help: \"reuse a register once its value is dead\" });\nconst emit = k.pass(\"regalloc+emit\").short(\"e\").whole(({ graph, edit, param }) => {\n  const order = graph.topo().filter((id) => graph.nodes.get(id).name !== \"kiln.input\");\n  const last = new Map();\n  order.forEach((id, i) => graph.nodes.get(id).args.forEach((a) => last.set(a, i)));\n  for (const o of graph.outputs) last.set(o.id, Infinity);\n  const reg = new Map(), free = [], code = [];\n  let nregs = 0;\n  const src = (id) => { const n = graph.nodes.get(id); return n.name === \"kiln.input\" ? \"%\" + n.attrs.name : \"r\" + reg.get(id); };\n  order.forEach((id, i) => {\n    const n = graph.nodes.get(id);\n    const ops = n.args.map(src);\n    if (param(REUSE)) for (const a of new Set(n.args)) if (last.get(a) === i && reg.has(a)) free.push(reg.get(a));\n    const r = free.length ? free.pop() : nregs++;\n    reg.set(id, r);\n    code.push(n.name === \"vm.imm\" ? `r${r} = imm ${n.attrs.value}` : `r${r} = ${n.attrs.opcode} ${ops.join(\", \")}`);\n  });\n  code.push(`ret ${src(graph.outputs[0].id)}`);\n  edit.replaceAll(edit.node(Asm.program, graph.inputs.map((x) => x.id), { code, regs: nregs, names: graph.inputs.map((x) => x.name) }));\n});\n\nconst pipe = k.pipeline(\"to-vm\")\n  .params(REUSE)\n  .ir(\"arith\", { dialects: [A] })\n  .pass(k.pass(\"lower\").short(\"l\").rules(lowerBin, lowerNeg, lowerConst))\n  .ir(\"vm\", { dialects: [Vm], strict: true })\n  .pass(emit)\n  .ir(\"asm\", { dialects: [Asm], strict: true })\n  .build();\n\nproject(\"poly\")\n  .pipeline(pipe)\n  .program(({ x, y }) => ({ out: x.mul(x).mul(x).mul(3).sub(x.mul(x).mul(2)).add(x.mul(y)).sub(7).neg() }), { x: k.scalar(), y: k.scalar() })\n  .data({ x: 1.5, y: -2 })\n  .check(\"the program computes what the source does\", ({ outputs, reference }) => Math.abs(outputs.out - reference.out) < 1e-9 || `got ${outputs.out}, want ${reference.out}`)\n  .check(\"registers are reused\", ({ run, graph }) => { const p = run.graph.find(\"asm.program\"); return !run.params.get(\"reuse\") || p.attrs.regs <= 4 || `${p.attrs.regs} registers`; });\n","notes":"# Lowering to a register VM\n\nCompilers are pipelines of lowerings: each pass rewrites the program into a lower-level dialect, and a checkpoint proves nothing was left behind. This project lowers arithmetic to three-address VM instructions, then a whole-graph pass allocates registers and emits a linear program.\n\n> **Try it** — compare the three levels: [arith](lab:ir?name=arith), [vm](lab:ir?name=vm), [asm](lab:ir?name=asm). Then [turn register reuse off](lab:param?name=reuse&value=false) and read the register count in the asm IR. [Turn it back on](lab:param?name=reuse&value=true).\n\n## Lowering with strict checkpoints\n\nEach `lower_*` rule rewrites a node **in place** with `n.lower(Vm.bin({ opcode }))`. The node keeps its id and its operands but changes its op. The `vm` checkpoint is `strict`: if any `arith.*` op survives, the compile stops with a `LoweringError` naming the node and the last rule that declined it. Delete `lowerNeg` to see one.\n\n## Register allocation: linear scan over a DAG\n\nA DAG has no control flow, so a topological order is a valid schedule, and register allocation reduces to *live ranges* in that order:\n\n1. A value is **live** from the instruction that defines it to its **last use**.\n2. Walk the instructions in order. Before allocating a destination, free the registers of operands whose last use is this instruction.\n3. Take a free register if there is one, otherwise a new one.\n\nThe number of registers needed equals the maximum number of simultaneously live values (the *register pressure*) along the chosen order. With reuse on, this polynomial needs 3 registers for 14 instructions; with reuse off, one per value.\n\n## Whole-graph passes\n\n`regalloc+emit` is a `.whole(...)` pass: it sees the entire graph at once, because allocation is a global decision. It builds a single `asm.program` node holding the instruction list, and `edit.replaceAll` makes it the output. The node's `eval` is a tiny interpreter, so even the final code is checked against the source: see the end-to-end check in [Code](lab:view?name=code).\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. The order of a DAG's topological sort changes the register pressure. Find an order for this program that needs only 2 registers, or prove it can't be done.\n2. Add an `fma` instruction (`r = a * b + c`) with a rule that fuses `bin mul` into a following `bin add` when the product has one use. How many instructions are saved?\n3. Make the VM have only 2 registers and add spills (`store r, slot` / `load slot`).\n\n## Further reading\n\n- Poletto & Sarkar, *Linear Scan Register Allocation* (TOPLAS 1999)\n- [Cornell CS 6120](https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs6120/2025fa/self-guided/): the lessons on SSA and register allocation\n"},{"id":"W-04","title":"Forks, knobs and sliders","group":"Basics","about":"a rule that offers several rewrites (a fork), a cost model that picks one per node, and sliders that move the choice","tries":["Tighten the tolerance: which exp() keeps the library call?","Pin TERMS=4 and read the check"],"text":"/** W-04 Forks, knobs and sliders\n *  group  Basics\n *  about  a rule that offers several rewrites (a fork), a cost model that picks one per node, and sliders that move the choice\n *  try    Tighten the tolerance: which exp() keeps the library call? | Pin TERMS=4 and read the check\n */\n// exp(t) can stay a library call (accurate, ~20 cycles here) or become a Taylor polynomial in Horner form\n// (2 ops per term, error ≈ e^R · R^(n+1)/(n+1)! on |t| ≤ R). Which is better depends on the node: exp(x/4)\n// sees a quarter of the range exp(x) does. The rule returns a FORK over the knob TERMS; the pipeline's\n// decide policy ranks the options at each node; Knobs shows every option and its score.\nconst X = k.dialect(\"ex\")\n  .op(\"const\", { attrs: { value: 0 }, traits: [\"pure\", \"const\"], infer: () => k.scalar(), eval: ({ value }) => value, print: \"{value}\" })\n  .op(\"add\", { args: [\"a\", \"b\"], traits: [\"pure\"], infer: ({ a }) => a.type, eval: ({ a, b }) => a + b, print: \"{a} + {b}\" })\n  .op(\"mul\", { args: [\"a\", \"b\"], traits: [\"pure\"], infer: ({ a }) => a.type, eval: ({ a, b }) => a * b, print: \"{a} * {b}\" })\n  .op(\"exp\", { args: [\"a\"], traits: [\"pure\"], infer: ({ a }) => a.type, eval: ({ a }) => Math.exp(a), print: \"exp({a})\" })\n  .lifts((v) => X.const({ value: v }))\n  .methods({ add: (a, b) => X.add(a, b), mul: (a, b) => X.mul(a, b), exp: (a) => X.exp(a) });\n\nconst TERMS = param.enum(\"TERMS\", [0, 3, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12], { off: 0, help: \"Taylor terms; 0 keeps the exp() call\" });\nconst RANGE = param.float(\"range\", { min: 0.25, max: 4, step: 0.25, default: 2, help: \"inputs satisfy |x| ≤ range\" });\nconst TOL = param.enum(\"tolerance\", [1e-2, 1e-4, 1e-6, 1e-8], { default: 1e-6, help: \"largest relative error allowed\" });\n\nconst fact = (n) => (n <= 1 ? 1 : n * fact(n - 1));\nconst taylor = k.rule(\"taylor_exp\")\n  .match(P.op(\"ex.exp\").as(\"e\"))\n  .rewrite(({ e }) => k.fork.over(TERMS, [3, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12], (n) => {\n    let p = X.const({ value: 1 / fact(n) });\n    for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) p = X.add(X.mul(p, e.arg(0)), 1 / fact(i));\n    return p;\n  }).keepOriginal());\n\n// the cost model: cycles, or Infinity if the error bound misses the tolerance\nconst scaleOf = (v) => (v.is(X.mul) && v.arg(1).isConst() ? Math.abs(v.arg(1).value) * scaleOf(v.arg(0)) : 1);\nconst cycles = k.decide.cost(({ knobs, node, run }) => {\n  const n = knobs.TERMS;\n  if (!n) return 20;\n  const R = run.params.get(RANGE) * scaleOf(node.arg(0));\n  const err = (R ** (n + 1) / fact(n + 1)) * Math.exp(R);\n  return err <= run.params.get(TOL) ? 2 * n : Infinity;\n}, { name: \"cycles\" });\n\nconst pipe = k.pipeline(\"approx\")\n  .params(RANGE, TOL)\n  .ir(\"ex\", { dialects: [X] })\n  .pass(k.pass(\"approximate\").short(\"a\").rules(taylor).knobs(TERMS))\n  .ir(\"approx\", { dialects: [X] })\n  .decide(k.decide.chain(k.decide.pinned(), cycles, k.decide.first()))\n  .build();\n\nproject(\"approx\")\n  .pipeline(pipe)\n  .program(({ x }) => ({ out: x.exp().add(x.mul(0.25).exp().mul(3)) }), { x: k.scalar() })\n  .data({ x: 1.25 })\n  .check(\"error within the tolerance on |x| ≤ range\", ({ run, graph }) => {\n    const R = run.params.get(RANGE), tol = run.params.get(TOL);\n    let worst = 0;\n    for (let i = 0; i <= 64; i++) { const x = -R + (2 * R * i) / 64; const want = graph.eval({ x }).out, got = run.graph.eval({ x }).out; worst = Math.max(worst, Math.abs(got - want) / Math.abs(want)); }\n    return worst <= tol * 1.01 || `worst relative error ${worst.toExponential(2)} > ${tol}`;\n  });\n","notes":"# Forks, knobs and sliders\n\nSome rewrites are not always wins. A **fork** lets a rule offer several rewrites and leave the choice to a **decide policy**: pins, measurements, or a cost model. Here, `exp(t)` can stay a library call or become a Taylor polynomial of a chosen length, and the right answer differs per call site.\n\n> **Try it** — open [Knobs](lab:view?name=knobs): two forks, each with every option scored. Then tighten the [tolerance to 1e-8](lab:param?name=tolerance&value=1e-8) and widen the [range to 4](lab:param?name=range&value=4), and watch the picks change. [Pin TERMS=4](lab:pin?TERMS=4) to force a choice, and read the check in [Code](lab:view?name=code). [Unpin](lab:unpin?name=TERMS).\n\n## The maths\n\nTruncating the Taylor series of $e^t$ after $n$ terms leaves the Lagrange remainder\n\n$$\\Big|e^t - \\sum_{i=0}^{n} \\frac{t^i}{i!}\\Big| \\;\\le\\; \\frac{|t|^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\, e^{|t|}$$\n\nso on $|t| \\le R$ the error bound is $\\varepsilon(n, R) = R^{n+1} e^R / (n+1)!$. In Horner form the polynomial costs $2n$ operations: one multiply and one add per term.\n\nThe cost model (`cycles`) prices each option:\n\n$$\\text{cost}(n) = \\begin{cases} 20 & n = 0 \\text{ (keep } \\exp) \\\\ 2n & \\varepsilon(n, R_\\text{node}) \\le \\text{tolerance} \\\\ \\infty & \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}$$\n\n$R_\\text{node}$ is the range *at that call site*. The program computes $e^x + 3e^{x/4}$, so the second call sees a quarter of the range: $R/4$.\n\nWith $R = 2$ and tolerance $10^{-6}$:\n\n- `exp(x)` needs 14 terms (28 operations) to meet the bound, more than the 12 the fork offers and more than the call costs (20), so the fork keeps the call.\n- `exp(x/4)` needs 8 terms (16 operations), which beats 20.\n\n## The system\n\n- `k.fork.over(TERMS, [3, 4, …], n => polynomial)` builds one option per value; `.keepOriginal()` adds \"leave it alone\" (`TERMS = 0`).\n- The pipeline's decide policy is `chain(pinned, cycles, first)`: a pin wins, otherwise the cost model, otherwise the first option.\n- A pinned knob narrows **every** fork that offers it. Per-node choices go through the kernel pins in [Knobs](lab:view?name=knobs).\n- The knobs a pass offers are declared with `.knobs(TERMS)`. They appear in the Code view's parameters as *auto* until you pin them.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Add a range-reduction option: $e^t = (e^{t/2^k})^{2^k}$, which costs $k$ squarings but shrinks $R$. When does it win?\n2. Replace the error bound by a measured error: evaluate each option on 64 points inside the cost function. What does that cost at compile time?\n3. Pin `TERMS=4`. Compute the error bound at each call site first, then predict which checks fail and by how much.\n"},{"id":"W-05","title":"RMSNorm, fused","group":"ML compiler","about":"one PyTorch-style op through six IRs into one row-mapped CUDA kernel, as in the CloudRift post","tries":["Why is this one kernel and not four?","Pin TPB=128 for the kernel and measure the difference"],"text":"/** W-05 RMSNorm, fused\n *  group  ML compiler\n *  about  one PyTorch-style op through six IRs into one row-mapped CUDA kernel, as in the CloudRift post\n *  try    Why is this one kernel and not four? | Pin TPB=128 for the kernel and measure the difference\n */\n// RMSNorm(x) = x / sqrt(mean(x²) + eps) * w. The frontend op decomposes into a reduction, a divide, an\n// rsqrt and two multiplies (tensor IR); lifting and fusion turn them into ONE loop kernel (loop IR); the\n// tile pass picks a GPU mapping from a fork of schedules (row-per-block here); the kernel pass makes it\n// cooperative (staged rows in shared memory, a tree reduction); emit prints CUDA and WGSL.\nconst rows = param.int(\"rows\", { min: 16, max: 4096, default: 1024, scale: \"log\", help: \"batch × sequence\" });\nconst dim = param.int(\"dim\", { min: 64, max: 8192, default: 4096, scale: \"log\", help: \"hidden size\" });\n\nproject(\"rmsnorm\")\n  .params(rows, dim)\n  .program(({ x }, { param }) => ({ y: N.rms_norm(x, std.weight(\"w\", [param(dim)]), { eps: 1e-6 }) }),\n           ({ param }) => ({ x: k.tensor([param(rows), param(dim)]) }))\n  .about(\"y = x · rsqrt(mean(x²) + ε) · w\");\n","notes":"# RMSNorm, fused\n\nOne PyTorch-style operator goes through all six IRs of the standard pipeline and comes out as one CUDA kernel. This is the example the CloudRift post builds its compiler around. Follow it level by level to see what each pass is for.\n\n> **Try it** — click through the levels in the [IR view](lab:ir?name=torch): torch → tensor → loop → tile → kernel → gpu. Then read the [CUDA](lab:kernel?name=k_rms_norm_reduce) and [measure](lab:measure).\n\n## The maths\n\n$$\\operatorname{RMSNorm}(x)_{ij} = \\frac{x_{ij}}{\\sqrt{\\frac1D \\sum_k x_{ik}^2 + \\varepsilon}}\\; w_j$$\n\nOne reduction per row (the mean of squares), then an elementwise scale. For $1024 \\times 4096$ fp32 inputs:\n\n- **Bytes:** read $x$ (16 MiB), read $w$ (16 KiB), write $y$ (16 MiB): 32 MiB.\n- **Work:** about $4D$ flop per row, so the intensity is about 1 flop/byte.\n- **Prediction:** memory-bound. $t \\approx 33.6\\text{ MB} / 1792\\text{ GB/s} + 2.5\\ \\mu s = 21.2\\ \\mu s$. The simulator says 24.3 µs.\n\n## The pipeline, level by level\n\n| IR | what it is | the pass that produces it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **torch** | `rms_norm(x, w)`: one frontend op with reference semantics | — |\n| **tensor** | about 13 primitive ops: `mul`, `reduce add`, `div`, `add`, `rsqrt`, broadcast views | `d` decomposition, `o` optimization |\n| **loop** | **one** `loop.kernel`: a loop nest with a reduction | `l` lift each op to a kernel, `f` fuse, `s` name |\n| **tile** | the loop kernel with a GPU mapping: block per row, 1024 threads, row staged in shared memory | `t` schedule (a fork, decided by the cost model) |\n| **kernel** | explicit threads: strided loops, shared-memory staging, a tree reduction with syncs | `k` materialize |\n| **gpu** | CUDA and WGSL source | `c` emit |\n\nThree ideas carry most of the weight.\n\n1. **Decomposition into a small primitive set.** Every frontend op becomes elementwise, reduction and view (index-map) ops. Later passes handle only those three kinds.\n2. **Fusion with loop-invariant code motion.** In the loop IR, every value is placed at the deepest loop it depends on. The row sum depends on the row only, so it is computed once per row, and `rsqrt` is hoisted out of the inner loop. Four kernels' worth of ops become one nest that reads $x$ once (twice with re-reads, served from shared memory).\n3. **Cooperative row reductions.** With `MAP=row`, a block of 1024 threads sweeps a row with a stride, each thread keeping a partial sum, then halves the partials in shared memory: 512, 256, … 1. That takes $\\log_2 1024 = 10$ steps with a barrier between each. See the *Kernel IR* tab in [Kernels](lab:view?name=kernels).\n\n## Try the knobs\n\n- [Pin TPB=128](lab:pin?MAP=row&TPB=128&measure): fewer threads per row, more blocks per SM. Faster or slower?\n- [Pin no staging](lab:pin?STAGE=none&measure): $x$ is read twice from memory instead of once.\n- [Unpin](lab:unpin).\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Count the rewrites per pass in [Trace](lab:view?name=trace). Which pass does the most work, and why?\n2. Compute the shared memory the staged schedule needs per block, and the blocks per SM it allows (128 KiB per SM on the 5090).\n3. Change the program to LayerNorm (`N.layer_norm`). How many reductions per row now, and does it still fuse into one kernel? (See W-13.)\n\n## Further reading\n\n- CloudRift, [*Building a GPU compiler from scratch*, part 1](https://www.cloudrift.ai/blog/building-gpu-compiler-from-scratch-1)\n- Mark Harris, *Optimizing Parallel Reduction in CUDA* (NVIDIA): the tree reduction\n"},{"id":"W-06","title":"Softmax in one kernel","group":"ML compiler","about":"max, exp-sum and normalize fuse into one row kernel with three sweeps: loop-invariant code motion at work","tries":["How many times does each row get read from DRAM?","Compare MAP=thread and MAP=row on the H100"],"text":"/** W-06 Softmax in one kernel\n *  group  ML compiler\n *  about  max, exp-sum and normalize fuse into one row kernel with three sweeps: loop-invariant code motion at work\n *  try    How many times does each row get read from DRAM? | Compare MAP=thread and MAP=row on the H100\n */\n// softmax(x) = exp(x − max(x)) / Σ exp(x − max(x)), row by row. Decomposed, that is two reductions and\n// three elementwise ops over a row. Fusion inlines them all into one Loop IR kernel; scheduling places each\n// computation at the deepest loop it depends on (LICM), which yields three sweeps per row: max, then sum,\n// then write. The tile pass maps a row to a block (MAP=row) with staging: the row is read from DRAM once.\nconst rows = param.int(\"rows\", { min: 16, max: 8192, default: 1024, scale: \"log\" });\nconst cols = param.int(\"cols\", { min: 32, max: 8192, default: 1024, scale: \"log\" });\n\nproject(\"softmax\")\n  .params(rows, cols)\n  .program(({ x }) => ({ y: N.softmax(x, -1) }), ({ param }) => ({ x: k.tensor([param(rows), param(cols)]) }))\n  .check(\"rows sum to 1\", ({ outputs }) => {\n    const y = outputs.y, C = y.shape[1];\n    for (let r = 0; r < y.shape[0]; r += Math.max(1, y.shape[0] >> 4)) { let s = 0; for (let c = 0; c < C; c++) s += y.data[r * C + c]; if (Math.abs(s - 1) > 1e-4) return `row ${r} sums to ${s}`; }\n    return true;\n  });\n","notes":"# Softmax in one kernel\n\nSoftmax is the classic case for fusion with reductions: two reductions and three elementwise ops per row become one kernel with three sweeps, reading the row from DRAM once.\n\n> **Try it** — read the [loop IR](lab:ir?name=loop) (three sweeps, in order), then the [CUDA](lab:view?name=kernels), then [measure](lab:measure).\n\n## The maths: the safe softmax\n\n$$\\operatorname{softmax}(x)_j = \\frac{e^{x_j - m}}{\\sum_k e^{x_k - m}}, \\qquad m = \\max_k x_k$$\n\nSubtracting the row maximum changes nothing mathematically (it cancels between numerator and denominator), but it keeps $e^{x_j - m} \\le 1$. Without it, $e^{x}$ overflows fp32 at $x > 88.7$, and logits beyond that are common.\n\nThe decomposition produces two reductions and three elementwise ops: max, subtract, exp, sum, divide. Fused with loop-invariant code motion, the loop IR becomes\n\n```text\nfor each row:\n  m = max_k x[k]                   # sweep 1\n  s = Σ_k exp(x[k] - m)            # sweep 2\n  for each j: y[j] = exp(x[j] - m) / s   # sweep 3\n```\n\nThree sweeps over the row, one kernel. With the row staged in shared memory, DRAM sees it once: $8\\,\\text{bytes} \\times$ elements in total.\n\n## The system\n\nThe tile pass maps a row to a block (`MAP=row`) with cooperative reductions for the max and the sum. Whether staging the row in shared memory pays depends on the row length and on occupancy: see W-23. For 1024 × 1024 the cost model's pick runs in 7.9 µs on the simulated RTX 5090, for 8 MiB of DRAM traffic.\n\n## Beyond: online softmax and FlashAttention\n\nTwo sweeps are enough: keep a running maximum $m$ and a running sum $s$, and rescale the sum whenever the maximum grows:\n\n$$m' = \\max(m, x_k), \\qquad s' = s \\cdot e^{m - m'} + e^{x_k - m'}$$\n\nThis combine is associative, so it fits a parallel tree reduction (Milakov & Gimelshein, 2018). It is also the key step of FlashAttention, which never materializes the attention matrix. This compiler's reducers are `add`, `max`, `min` and `mul`; a pair-valued reducer is the extension that would enable both.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Remove the max subtraction (write softmax by hand with `N.exp` and `N.sum`) and feed it inputs around 100. What does the end-to-end check say?\n2. How many bytes does the unstaged schedule read per row? [Pin STAGE=none](lab:pin?STAGE=none&measure) and compare the DRAM column. [Unpin](lab:unpin).\n3. Write the online-softmax recurrence for a tree reduction: how do two partial results $(m_a, s_a)$ and $(m_b, s_b)$ combine?\n\n## Further reading\n\n- Milakov & Gimelshein, *Online normalizer calculation for softmax* (2018)\n- Dao et al., [FlashAttention](https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.14135)\n"},{"id":"W-07","title":"GELU, pointwise","group":"ML compiler","about":"a chain of elementwise ops becomes one thread-per-element kernel; COARSEN and TPB are the knobs","tries":["Why is this kernel bound by DRAM?","Pin COARSEN=4 and compare"],"text":"/** W-07 GELU, pointwise\n *  group  ML compiler\n *  about  a chain of elementwise ops becomes one thread-per-element kernel; COARSEN and TPB are the knobs\n *  try    Why is this kernel bound by DRAM? | Pin COARSEN=4 and compare\n */\n// GELU (tanh approximation) on a bias-shifted input: y = gelu(x + b) · s. Seven elementwise ops, one\n// kernel: every thread reads x[i] and b[j] once and writes y[i] once. Arithmetic intensity is ~2 flop/B,\n// so on any GPU this kernel lives under the memory roof — check the Roofline in Perf.\nconst n = param.int(\"tokens\", { min: 64, max: 65536, default: 8192, scale: \"log\" });\nconst d = param.int(\"hidden\", { min: 64, max: 8192, default: 2048, scale: \"log\" });\n\nproject(\"gelu\")\n  .params(n, d)\n  .program(({ x }, { param }) => {\n    const b = std.weight(\"bias\", [param(d)]);\n    return { y: N.mul(N.gelu(N.add(x, b)), 0.5) };\n  }, ({ param }) => ({ x: k.tensor([param(n), param(d)]) }));\n","notes":"# GELU, pointwise\n\nThe simplest GPU kernel shape: every output element depends only on the same element of the inputs. One thread per element (or per few elements), no shared memory, no synchronization. The only question is how fast the bytes move.\n\n> **Try it** — [measure](lab:measure), then compare thread-mapping options in [Knobs](lab:view?name=knobs): [64 threads per block](lab:pin?TPB=64&COARSEN=1&measure), [1024](lab:pin?TPB=1024&COARSEN=1&measure), [4 elements per thread](lab:pin?TPB=256&COARSEN=4&measure). [Unpin](lab:unpin).\n\n## The maths\n\n$$y = 0.5 \\cdot \\operatorname{GELU}(x + b), \\qquad \\operatorname{GELU}(u) = \\tfrac12 u \\Big(1 + \\tanh\\big(\\sqrt{2/\\pi}\\,(u + 0.044715\\,u^3)\\big)\\Big)$$\n\nAbout 15 flop per element against 8 bytes (read $x$, write $y$; $b$ is one row, cached): roughly 2 flop/byte. That is well below every GPU's ridge point (W-18), so the kernel is memory-bound:\n\n$$t \\approx \\frac{8 \\cdot 8192 \\cdot 2048\\ \\text{B}}{1792\\ \\text{GB/s}} + 2.5\\ \\mu s = 77.4\\ \\mu s \\quad\\text{(simulated: 77.6 µs)}$$\n\n## The system: thread mapping\n\n`MAP=thread` gives each thread `COARSEN` consecutive outputs; `TPB` threads form a block.\n\n- **Coalescing.** Consecutive threads touch consecutive addresses, so a warp's 32 loads fall into 4 sectors of 32 bytes: perfect. *sectors / request* in Perf should read 4.0.\n- **Coarsening.** More elements per thread mean fewer threads and more independent loads per thread: more instruction-level parallelism, less index arithmetic. For a memory-bound kernel the gain is small, because the bottleneck is DRAM.\n- **Block size.** Small blocks hit the 24-blocks-per-SM limit before the 1536-threads limit (W-23); 64 threads per block or more reach full occupancy.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Why is coarsening by 4 not 4× faster? What would it take for coarsening to matter?\n2. Change the scale to a learned per-column vector. How many bytes per element now?\n3. Look at the generated WGSL in [Kernels](lab:view?name=kernels): how does it compute the global index when the grid has more than 65,535 blocks?\n"},{"id":"W-08","title":"Linear layer, tiled","group":"ML compiler","about":"y = x·Wᵀ becomes a shared-memory tiled matmul with register blocking: BM, BN, BK, TM, TN are the fork","tries":["Which tile shape did the cost model pick, and is it the fastest measured?","Tune with a budget of 48"],"text":"/** W-08 Linear layer, tiled\n *  group  ML compiler\n *  about  y = x·Wᵀ becomes a shared-memory tiled matmul with register blocking: BM, BN, BK, TM, TN are the fork\n *  try    Which tile shape did the cost model pick, and is it the fastest measured? | Tune with a budget of 48\n */\n// A contraction: y[m, n] = Σ_k x[m, k] · W[n, k]. The tile pass recognizes the contraction in the Loop IR\n// and offers MAP=tiled schedules: each block computes a BM×BN tile, walking K in BK slices staged in\n// shared memory; each thread holds a TM×TN register tile. The fork has dozens of legal options; the cost\n// model ranks them and Tune measures them. Coalescing-aware loads keep sectors per request near 4.\nconst M = param.int(\"M\", { min: 64, max: 8192, default: 1024, scale: \"log\", help: \"tokens\" });\nconst K = param.int(\"K\", { min: 64, max: 8192, default: 1024, scale: \"log\", help: \"in features\" });\nconst Nf = param.int(\"N\", { min: 64, max: 8192, default: 1024, scale: \"log\", help: \"out features\" });\n\nproject(\"linear\")\n  .params(M, K, Nf)\n  .program(({ x }, { param }) => ({ y: N.linear(x, std.weight(\"W\", [param(Nf), param(K)])) }),\n           ({ param }) => ({ x: k.tensor([param(M), param(K)]) }));\n","notes":"# Linear layer, tiled\n\n$y = x W^\\top$: the operation that dominates transformer FLOPs. The tile pass recognizes a **contraction** in the loop IR and offers shared-memory tiled schedules, hundreds of them. This is the compiler's view of what W-21 walks through by hand.\n\n> **Try it** — open the `schedule` fork in [Knobs](lab:view?name=knobs): 645 legal tile shapes, ranked by the cost model. [Measure](lab:measure), then [tune for 32 programs](lab:tune?strategy=spmcts&budget=32) and see whether the measured best differs from the model's pick.\n\n## The maths\n\n$$y_{mn} = \\sum_k x_{mk}\\, W_{nk}, \\qquad W = 2MNK, \\qquad I_\\text{max} = \\frac{2MNK}{4(MK + NK + MN)}$$\n\nAt $M = N = K = 1024$ that is 2.1 GFLOP and up to 171 flop/byte: compute-heavy, *if* the schedule reuses data on-chip.\n\n## The system\n\nThe tile pass analyzes the loop nest: two operands indexed by $(m, k)$ and $(n, k)$, a sum over $k$, an output over $(m, n)$. Each `MAP=tiled` option fixes:\n\n| knob | meaning | effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BM × BN` | output tile per block | global traffic $\\propto 1/B_M + 1/B_N$; fewer blocks |\n| `BK` | depth of each shared-memory slab | shared memory $= 4 B_K (B_M + B_N)$ bytes |\n| `TM × TN` | outputs per thread (register tile) | shared-memory reads per FMA $= (T_M + T_N)/(T_M T_N)$ |\n\nLegality: at most 1024 threads per block, at most 48 KiB of static shared memory, and at least one warp of threads.\n\nThe kernel each option produces is in [Kernels](lab:view?name=kernels): a loop over $k$-slabs, cooperative tile loads, a barrier, an unrolled register-tile update, a barrier, and finally the epilogue. In W-09 and W-16 the epilogue includes fused activations.\n\nThe cost model's pick runs at about 18 TFLOP/s on the simulated RTX 5090 (119.8 µs), limited by shared-memory bandwidth. W-21 explains why and what comes next.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. [Make $M = 64$](lab:param?name=M&value=64&measure) (a small batch) and compare the model's pick and its time with $M = 1024$. W-20 explores this axis in depth.\n2. Add a bias: `N.linear(x, W, b)`. Where does the bias add end up in the kernel?\n3. Switch to the [Orin](lab:device?id=sim:orin&measure) (8 SMs): which tile shape does the model pick there, and why does a GPU with fewer SMs prefer bigger tiles? (W-24.)\n"},{"id":"W-09","title":"MLP and the blowup guard","group":"ML compiler","about":"fusion stops where it would recompute work: a GELU or SwiGLU MLP keeps its matmuls apart","tries":["Why are there exactly these kernels? Show me the guard's decline","Switch act to swiglu"],"text":"/** W-09 MLP and the blowup guard\n *  group  ML compiler\n *  about  fusion stops where it would recompute work: a GELU or SwiGLU MLP keeps its matmuls apart\n *  try    Why are there exactly these kernels? Show me the guard's decline | Switch act to swiglu\n */\n// Fusing a producer into its consumer inlines the producer's whole computation at every use. For an\n// elementwise producer that is free; for a matmul feeding another matmul it multiplies the work by the\n// hidden size. The fusion rule's guard compares the work before and after (workOf, with a memory weight)\n// and declines: Trace → Declined shows \"would multiply work by …\". The activation still fuses into the\n// first matmul's epilogue.\nconst act = param.enum(\"act\", [\"gelu\", \"swiglu\"], { default: \"gelu\" });\nconst T_ = param.int(\"tokens\", { min: 16, max: 4096, default: 512, scale: \"log\" });\nconst D = param.int(\"dim\", { min: 64, max: 4096, default: 512, scale: \"log\" });\nconst H = param.int(\"hidden\", { min: 64, max: 8192, default: 1024, scale: \"log\" });\n\nproject(\"mlp\")\n  .params(act, T_, D, H)\n  .program(({ x }, { param }) => {\n    const d = param(D), h = param(H);\n    if (param(act) === \"gelu\") return { y: N.linear(N.gelu(N.linear(x, std.weight(\"up\", [h, d]))), std.weight(\"down\", [d, h])) };\n    const g = N.silu(N.linear(x, std.weight(\"gate\", [h, d])));\n    return { y: N.linear(N.mul(g, N.linear(x, std.weight(\"up\", [h, d]))), std.weight(\"down\", [d, h])) };\n  }, ({ param }) => ({ x: k.tensor([param(T_), param(D)]) }))\n  .check(\"matmuls stay separate kernels\", ({ run }) => {\n    const ks = run.graph.findAll(\"gpu.kernel\").length;\n    return ks >= 2 || `only ${ks} kernel: fusion recomputed a matmul`;\n  });\n","notes":"# MLP and the blowup guard\n\nFusion is not always a win. Fusing one matmul into another would **recompute** the first one for every element of the second. A guard in the fusion pass compares the work before and after, and declines. This project shows the guard at work, and where fusion still applies.\n\n> **Try it** — in [Trace](lab:view?name=trace), turn on *Declined*: `merge_loop_ops` declines with *blowup: fused work … > unfused …*. Then switch the activation to [SwiGLU](lab:param?name=act&value=swiglu) and back to [GELU](lab:param?name=act&value=gelu).\n\n## The maths of recomputation\n\nTake $h = \\operatorname{GELU}(xW_\\text{up}^\\top)$ with $h \\in \\mathbb{R}^{T \\times H}$, and $y = hW_\\text{down}^\\top$ with $y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{T \\times D}$. The second matmul reads every $h_{tk}$ once **per output column**, $D$ times in all. Fusing a producer into it replaces each of those reads by the producer's own computation:\n\n| fusion tried | each $h_{tk}$ then costs | work ratio (fused / unfused) | verdict |\n|---|---|---:|---|\n| GELU's elementwise ops into the second matmul (a *prologue*) | GELU recomputed $D$ times | 1.5–2.4× | declined |\n| the first matmul into the second | a length-$D$ dot product, recomputed $D$ times | 171–256× | declined |\n| GELU into the first matmul's *epilogue* | GELU once, as the output is written | < 1× | **accepted** |\n\nThe matmul-into-matmul ratio is easy to derive. With $c$ the cost of one multiply-add including its two loads,\n\n$$W_\\text{fused} \\approx \\underbrace{T D H}_{\\text{reads of } h} \\cdot \\underbrace{D\\,c}_{\\text{one dot product}}, \\qquad W_\\text{unfused} \\approx 2 \\cdot TDH\\,c \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\frac{W_\\text{fused}}{W_\\text{unfused}} \\approx \\frac{D}{2} = 256$$\n\nFusion is only free when the producer's value is used about once per element, or when recomputing it is cheaper than a round trip through memory.\n\n## The guard\n\n`merge_loop_ops` has a guard: fuse only if $\\text{work}(\\text{fused}) \\le \\text{work}(\\text{producer}) + \\text{work}(\\text{consumer})$. Work counts flops plus 12 units per memory access (`W_MEM`), so inlining a cheap elementwise producer that is read once still wins: it removes a store and a load. The [Trace](lab:view?name=trace) shows 13 declined steps with their numbers, e.g. *blowup: fused work 10,472,128,512 > unfused 7,001,866,240* (a piece of GELU into the second matmul) and *fused work 3,581,468,999,680 > unfused 13,976,469,504* (matmul into matmul).\n\nThe result is 2 kernels: `k_linear_gelu_reduce` (32.2 µs) and `k_linear_reduce` (44.4 µs) on the simulated RTX 5090.\n\nThe pass also runs **consumers first** (`order(\"reverse\")`). Fusing in producer order could merge the wrong pair first and leave a worse partition.\n\n## SwiGLU\n\n$$\\operatorname{SwiGLU}(x) = \\big(\\operatorname{SiLU}(xW_g^\\top) \\odot xW_u^\\top\\big) W_d^\\top$$\n\nTwo matmuls read the same $x$; their elementwise product feeds the third. Count the kernels after the switch: which fusions does the guard accept now?\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Change `W_MEM` in `std/loop.js` to 1. Which fusions does the guard accept now, and is the compiled program faster or slower?\n2. Write the work estimate for fusing a row softmax into a following matmul over the same rows. Is that fusion profitable? (It is the heart of FlashAttention.)\n3. Find the declined steps' node ids in Trace, then look those nodes up in the [loop IR](lab:ir?name=loop).\n"},{"id":"W-10","title":"Attention","group":"ML compiler","about":"softmax(QKᵀ/√d)·V for several heads: batched tiled matmuls around a fused scale-and-softmax","tries":["Add a causal mask with N.select and N.lt","Which kernel dominates, and what bounds it?"],"text":"/** W-10 Attention\n *  group  ML compiler\n *  about  softmax(QKᵀ/√d)·V for several heads: batched tiled matmuls around a fused scale-and-softmax\n *  try    Add a causal mask with N.select and N.lt | Which kernel dominates, and what bounds it?\n */\n// Scaled dot-product attention, batched over heads. The scale fuses into the softmax kernel; the two\n// matmuls are batched contractions the tile pass maps like W-08 (with the head as a grid dimension).\nconst heads = param.int(\"heads\", { min: 1, max: 32, default: 8 });\nconst seq = param.int(\"seq\", { min: 16, max: 2048, default: 256, scale: \"log\" });\nconst dk = param.int(\"d_head\", { min: 16, max: 128, default: 64, scale: \"log\" });\n\nproject(\"attention\")\n  .params(heads, seq, dk)\n  .program(({ q, kk, v }, { param }) => {\n    const s = N.mul(N.matmul(q, N.transpose(kk)), 1 / Math.sqrt(param(dk)));\n    return { out: N.matmul(N.softmax(s, -1), v) };\n  }, ({ param }) => { const t = k.tensor([param(heads), param(seq), param(dk)]); return { q: t, kk: t, v: t }; });\n","notes":"# Attention\n\nScaled dot-product attention, batched over heads:\n\n$$\\operatorname{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \\operatorname{softmax}\\!\\Big(\\frac{QK^\\top}{\\sqrt{d}}\\Big)V$$\n\nThe compiler produces three kernels: $S = QK^\\top$ (tiled), the scale fused into the softmax (a row kernel), and $PV$ (tiled). Here is what that costs, and what FlashAttention changes.\n\n| sequence $L$ | $QK^\\top$ | scale + softmax | $PV$ | total |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 256 | 6.2 µs | 5.0 µs | 9.5 µs | 20.8 µs |\n| 1024 | 41.9 | 40.6 | 54.5 | 137 |\n| 2048 | 168 | 153 | 218 | 539 |\n\nSimulated RTX 5090, 8 heads, $d = 64$. From 256 to 2048 the sequence grows 8× and the time 26×; with launch overheads amortized, it tends to the $64\\times$ of an $L^2$ algorithm. At $L = 2048$ the softmax kernel alone moves 256 MiB through DRAM: it reads the score matrix once and writes it once.\n\n> **Try it** — [measure](lab:measure), then grow the [sequence to 1024](lab:param?name=seq&value=1024&measure) and [2048](lab:param?name=seq&value=2048&measure) and watch the DRAM column of the middle kernel. [Back to 256](lab:param?name=seq&value=256).\n\n## The maths: quadratic memory\n\nWith $h$ heads, sequence length $L$ and head dimension $d$:\n\n| | flops | bytes the score matrix moves |\n|---|---|---|\n| $S = QK^\\top$ | $2hL^2d$ | write $4hL^2$ |\n| softmax | $\\approx 5hL^2$ | read and write $4hL^2$ each |\n| $PV$ | $2hL^2d$ | read $4hL^2$ |\n\nThe score matrix $S \\in \\mathbb{R}^{h \\times L \\times L}$ goes through DRAM about four times. Its size grows with $L^2$, while $Q$, $K$, $V$ and the output only grow with $L$. At $h = 8$, $L = 2048$ that is 128 MiB of scores, for 3 × 4 MiB of inputs. For long sequences attention is dominated by moving a matrix that nobody needs to keep.\n\n## FlashAttention in one paragraph\n\nTile $Q$ by rows. For each tile, stream $K$ and $V$ tile by tile, computing a block of $S$ in shared memory. Update a running row max, a running softmax denominator and a running output with the online-softmax rescaling (W-06). Nothing of size $L^2$ ever reaches DRAM. The flops are the same; the bytes drop from $O(hL^2)$ to $O(hLd)$.\n\nThis compiler cannot do that yet. It needs a pair-valued reducer (running max and sum), and fusion *across* a contraction, which the blowup guard currently declines (see the declined steps in [Trace](lab:view?name=trace)). Both are good projects.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Add a causal mask as a fourth input, `mask: k.tensor([seq, seq])` (0 on and below the diagonal, $-10^9$ above), and add it to the scores before the softmax. Does it fuse into the softmax kernel? How many bytes does reading it cost at $L = 2048$, and how would computing the mask from the indices instead (`row < col`) avoid them?\n2. At which sequence length does the score matrix exceed the RTX 5090's 96 MB of L2?\n3. Compute the arithmetic intensity of the $QK^\\top$ kernel for $d = 64$. Why is attention with small heads harder to make compute-bound than a big linear layer?\n\n## Further reading\n\n- Vaswani et al., *Attention Is All You Need* (2017)\n- Dao et al., [FlashAttention](https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.14135); Milakov & Gimelshein, *Online normalizer calculation for softmax*\n"},{"id":"W-11","title":"N-body forces","group":"Write programs","about":"an all-pairs physics kernel written with broadcasting: the compiler finds a reduction over particles","tries":["Is this compute-bound or memory-bound, and why?","Add a softening slider and a mass per particle"],"text":"/** W-11 N-body forces\n *  group  Write programs\n *  about  an all-pairs physics kernel written with broadcasting: the compiler finds a reduction over particles\n *  try    Is this compute-bound or memory-bound, and why? | Add a softening slider and a mass per particle\n */\n// For every particle i: a_i = Σ_j (p_j − p_i) / (|p_j − p_i|² + ε)^{3/2}. Written as tensor code: the pair\n// differences are a broadcast (n,1,3) − (1,n,3), the distance is a reduction over xyz, and the force sum\n// is a reduction over j. The compiler never materializes the n×n×3 tensor: it fuses into reductions.\nconst n = param.int(\"particles\", { min: 64, max: 8192, default: 512, scale: \"log\" });\nconst soft = param.float(\"softening\", { min: 0.001, max: 0.5, step: 0.001, default: 0.01 });\n\nproject(\"nbody\")\n  .params(n, soft)\n  .program(({ p }, { param }) => {\n    const N_ = param(n);\n    const d = N.sub(N.reshape(p, [1, N_, 3]), N.reshape(p, [N_, 1, 3]));          // d[i, j] = p_j − p_i\n    const r2 = N.add(N.sum(N.square(d), -1), param(soft));                       // (n, n, 1)\n    const inv = N.pow(r2, -1.5);\n    return { a: N.sum(N.mul(d, inv), 1, false) };                                // Σ_j → (n, 3)\n  }, ({ param }) => ({ p: k.tensor([param(n), 3]) }))\n  .check(\"forces sum to ~zero (Newton's third law)\", ({ outputs }) => {\n    const a = outputs.a.data; const s = [0, 0, 0];\n    for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) s[i % 3] += a[i];\n    const scale = a.reduce((m, x) => Math.max(m, Math.abs(x)), 0) * Math.sqrt(a.length);\n    return s.every((x) => Math.abs(x) < 1e-3 * scale) || `net force ${s.map((x) => x.toExponential(2)).join(\", \")}`;\n  });\n","notes":"# N-body forces\n\nNot every GPU kernel is a neural-network layer. This project computes the gravitational pull of every particle on every other particle — an $O(n^2)$ all-pairs interaction — written with plain broadcasting. The compiler finds the reductions, maps the pairwise distances to a tiled kernel, and refuses one tempting fusion.\n\n> **Try it** — [measure](lab:measure) at 512 particles, then at [2048](lab:param?name=particles&value=2048&measure) and [8192](lab:param?name=particles&value=8192&measure). Watch which bound the kernels hit. [Back to 512](lab:param?name=particles&value=512).\n\n## The maths\n\nWith unit masses and $G = 1$, particle $i$ accelerates towards every other particle $j$:\n\n$$a_i = \\sum_j \\frac{p_j - p_i}{\\big(\\lVert p_j - p_i \\rVert^2 + \\varepsilon\\big)^{3/2}}$$\n\nThe **softening** $\\varepsilon$ keeps the force finite when two particles nearly coincide, and makes the $j = i$ term zero instead of $0/0$. Because the pair force is antisymmetric ($F_{ij} = -F_{ji}$), the total $\\sum_i a_i$ is zero up to rounding: Newton's third law, which the project's check tests.\n\nThe program builds the pairwise differences by broadcasting: `reshape(p, [1, n, 3]) − reshape(p, [n, 1, 3])` is an $n \\times n \\times 3$ tensor that is never stored. Views compose into index maps, so `d[i, j, c]` is just `p[j, c] − p[i, c]` inside a loop.\n\n**Work.** About 20 flops per pair: $20n^2$ in all. Bytes: the positions, $12n$. The intensity grows linearly with $n$, so all-pairs kernels are classic compute-bound workloads, *if* the positions are reused from on-chip memory.\n\n## What the compiler makes\n\nTwo kernels (simulated RTX 5090):\n\n| particles | `k_ew_reduce_reduce`: $r^2_{ij}$ | `k_ew_reduce_reduce_2`: $a_i$ | total |\n|---:|---|---|---:|\n| 512 | 3.3 µs, tiled, launch-bound | 5.1 µs, row per (particle, coordinate) | 8.4 µs |\n| 2048 | 17.5 µs, L2-bound | 41.0 µs, L2-bound | 58.4 µs |\n| 8192 | 376 µs, DRAM-bound | 1093 µs, DRAM-bound | 1469 µs |\n\n1. **The squared distances** $r^2_{ij} = \\sum_c (p_{jc} - p_{ic})^2$ form an $n \\times n$ matrix with a reduction over 3 coordinates. The tile pass recognizes the shape of a contraction (output $(i, j)$, reduction over $c$) and offers shared-memory tiles.\n2. **The accelerations** $a_{ic} = \\sum_j d_{ijc} \\cdot (r^2_{ij} + \\varepsilon)^{-3/2}$: a row reduction over $j$ for each output $(i, c)$.\n\n**The declined fusion.** Inlining $r^2$ into the second kernel would avoid writing and re-reading the $n \\times n$ matrix. But the second kernel reads each $r^2_{ij}$ three times (once per coordinate $c$), so fusing would compute every distance and every `pow` three times. The guard declines: *fused work 48,777,216 > unfused 40,912,896* (see *Declined* in [Trace](lab:view?name=trace)). At $n = 8192$ that decision costs dearly: the $r^2$ matrix is 256 MiB and goes through DRAM twice, which is why both kernels end up DRAM-bound.\n\n## How it should be done\n\nThe classic GPU n-body kernel (*GPU Gems 3*, chapter 31) tiles over $j$: each block loads a tile of positions into shared memory, and each thread accumulates the force on its own particle from all of them. Nothing of size $n^2$ is stored; each position is read from DRAM $n/\\text{tile}$ times; the kernel runs near peak FLOP/s. Here that would need a reduction whose output has 3 components per thread — a vector-valued reducer, the same extension as online softmax (W-06) and Welford (W-22).\n\n> **Note** — the tile pass only offers `MAP=tiled` when every value the reduction loop reads is either a tile operand or computed inside the loop. A hoisted value like $p_i$ inside $\\sum_j (p_j - p_i)\\,w_{ij}$ would have no home in a tiled kernel. That legality rule was added after this very program broke the tiled materializer at 2048 particles.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Compute the arithmetic intensity of the ideal (tiled-over-$j$) kernel at $n = 8192$ and place it on the RTX 5090's roofline (W-18).\n2. Rewrite the program so the second kernel reads $r^2$ once: compute the scalar weight $w_{ij} = (r^2_{ij} + \\varepsilon)^{-3/2}$ as its own output first. Does the fusion guard now accept a different partition?\n3. The check accepts a net force below $10^{-3} \\cdot \\max_i |a_i| \\cdot \\sqrt{3n}$. Why scale the tolerance with the largest force, and why with $\\sqrt{n}$ rather than $n$? (Hint: rounding errors of a sum behave like a random walk.)\n\n## Further reading\n\n- Nyland, Harris, Prins, *Fast N-Body Simulation with CUDA*, GPU Gems 3, chapter 31\n- Barnes & Hut (1986): the $O(n \\log n)$ tree algorithm that replaces all-pairs at large $n$\n"},{"id":"W-12","title":"Box blur, a stencil","group":"Write programs","about":"nine shifted slices summed: views compose into index maps, so the whole stencil is one kernel","tries":["Make the radius a slider","Why is sectors per request above 4 here?"],"text":"/** W-12 Box blur, a stencil\n *  group  Write programs\n *  about  nine shifted slices summed: views compose into index maps, so the whole stencil is one kernel\n *  try    Make the radius a slider | Why is sectors per request above 4 here?\n */\n// out[y, x] = mean of img[y + dy, x + dx] for dy, dx in 0..2r. Each shifted slice is a view (an index map,\n// not a copy); the adds fuse; the kernel reads each input pixel from up to nine threads (L2 hits).\nconst H = param.int(\"height\", { min: 32, max: 4096, default: 1024, scale: \"log\" });\nconst W = param.int(\"width\", { min: 32, max: 4096, default: 1024, scale: \"log\" });\nconst r = param.int(\"radius\", { min: 1, max: 3, default: 1 });\n\nproject(\"blur\")\n  .params(H, W, r)\n  .program(({ img }, { param }) => {\n    const h = param(H), w = param(W), k2 = 2 * param(r) + 1;\n    let acc = null;\n    for (let dy = 0; dy < k2; dy++) for (let dx = 0; dx < k2; dx++) {\n      const s = N.slice(N.slice(img, 0, dy, dy + h), 1, dx, dx + w);\n      acc = acc ? N.add(acc, s) : s;\n    }\n    return { out: N.mul(acc, 1 / (k2 * k2)) };\n  }, ({ param }) => ({ img: k.tensor([param(H) + 2 * param(r), param(W) + 2 * param(r)]) }));\n","notes":"# Box blur, a stencil\n\nA stencil — each output pixel averages its neighbourhood — written as a sum of shifted slices of the input. The slices are **views**: index maps, not copies. They compose and fuse, so the whole stencil is one kernel that never materializes a shifted image.\n\n> **Try it** — [measure](lab:measure), then grow the [radius to 2](lab:param?name=radius&value=2&measure) and [3](lab:param?name=radius&value=3&measure). [Back to 1](lab:param?name=radius&value=1).\n\n## The maths\n\n$$\\text{out}_{yx} = \\frac{1}{(2r+1)^2} \\sum_{dy=0}^{2r} \\sum_{dx=0}^{2r} \\text{img}_{y+dy,\\,x+dx}$$\n\nThe input is padded by $r$ on each side, so `slice(slice(img, 0, dy, dy+H), 1, dx, dx+W)` is the window shifted by $(dy, dx)$. In the Tensor IR each slice is a `view` with the index map $(y, x) \\mapsto (y + dy, x + dx)$. The optimizer folds view-of-view into one map, and fusion inlines all $(2r+1)^2$ of them into one loop body. Read the [loop IR](lab:ir?name=loop): nine loads of `in0` at different offsets, eight adds, one multiply.\n\n## The system: L2 does the reuse\n\nEach input pixel is read by $(2r+1)^2$ different threads. Nothing in the kernel shares those reads explicitly, but neighbouring threads run at nearly the same time, so the repeats hit the L2 cache. The kernel is **L2-bound**, not DRAM-bound:\n\n| radius | taps | L2 traffic | time (simulated RTX 5090, 1024 × 1024) |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | 9 | 48 MiB | 12.1 µs |\n| 2 | 25 | 124 MiB | 27.5 µs |\n| 3 | 49 | 242 MiB | 51.1 µs |\n\nThe time grows with the number of taps, while DRAM traffic stays near the 8 MiB minimum (read the image once, write the result once). Two details are visible in [Perf](lab:view?name=perf):\n\n- **Sectors per request ≈ 4.9, not 4.** A warp's 32 consecutive loads start at offset $dx$, so 128 bytes straddle 5 of the 32-byte sectors whenever $dx \\bmod 8 \\ne 0$. Misaligned access wastes about 20% of each transfer.\n- **Intensity ≈ 1 flop/byte** against L2: no schedule choice can make this kernel compute-bound.\n\n## How it should be done\n\nProduction stencils stage a tile plus its **halo** (the $r$-wide border) in shared memory: each block loads $(B + 2r)^2$ pixels once and computes $B^2$ outputs from shared memory. A separable blur goes further: a box filter is a horizontal pass followed by a vertical pass, $2(2r+1)$ taps instead of $(2r+1)^2$. Both are transformations this compiler doesn't do: the first needs a 2-D staging schedule, the second an algebraic rewrite of the program.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Write the separable version as two programs-in-one: a horizontal blur into a temporary, then a vertical blur. Does the compiler fuse them back into one kernel? Should it?\n2. For radius 3, how many bytes would a halo-tiled kernel with 32 × 32 output tiles read from L2 per output pixel?\n3. Change the padding so the slices start at multiples of 8 floats. Does *sectors per request* drop to 4?\n"},{"id":"W-13","title":"LayerNorm with a residual","group":"Write programs","about":"y = LayerNorm(x + r)·g + b: the residual add fuses into the norm's row kernel, so x + r is never stored","tries":["Return x + r as a second output: what happens to the kernel count?","Compare with W-05 on the A100"],"text":"/** W-13 LayerNorm with a residual\n *  group  Write programs\n *  about  y = LayerNorm(x + r)·g + b: the residual add fuses into the norm's row kernel, so x + r is never stored\n *  try    Return x + r as a second output: what happens to the kernel count? | Compare with W-05 on the A100\n */\nconst rows = param.int(\"rows\", { min: 16, max: 8192, default: 2048, scale: \"log\" });\nconst dim = param.int(\"dim\", { min: 64, max: 8192, default: 1024, scale: \"log\" });\n\nproject(\"ln-residual\")\n  .params(rows, dim)\n  .program(({ x, res }, { param }) => {\n    const d = param(dim);\n    return { y: N.layer_norm(N.add(x, res), std.weight(\"g\", [d], { init: \"ones\" }), std.weight(\"b\", [d], { init: \"zeros\" }), { eps: 1e-5 }) };\n  }, ({ param }) => { const t = k.tensor([param(rows), param(dim)]); return { x: t, res: t }; })\n  .check(\"one kernel\", ({ run }) => run.graph.findAll(\"gpu.kernel\").length === 1 || \"the residual did not fuse\")\n  .check(\"each row has mean 0 and variance 1\", ({ outputs }) => {\n    const y = outputs.y, D = y.shape[1];\n    for (let r = 0; r < y.shape[0]; r += Math.max(1, y.shape[0] >> 3)) {\n      let m = 0, v = 0; for (let j = 0; j < D; j++) m += y.data[r * D + j]; m /= D;\n      for (let j = 0; j < D; j++) v += (y.data[r * D + j] - m) ** 2; v /= D;\n      if (Math.abs(m) > 1e-3 || Math.abs(v - 1) > 1e-2) return `row ${r}: mean ${m.toFixed(4)}, variance ${v.toFixed(4)}`;\n    }\n    return true;\n  });\n","notes":"# LayerNorm with a residual\n\nEvery transformer block ends with $y = \\operatorname{LayerNorm}(x + r)$: the sub-layer's output added to the residual stream, then normalized. Unfused, the sum $x + r$ is written to memory and read back. Fused, it lives in registers and shared memory for the duration of one row.\n\n> **Try it** — [measure](lab:measure) and look at the DRAM column in [Perf](lab:view?name=perf), then read the single kernel in [Kernels](lab:view?name=kernels).\n\n## The maths\n\n$$u = x + r, \\qquad \\mu = \\frac1D \\sum_j u_j, \\qquad \\sigma^2 = \\frac1D \\sum_j (u_j - \\mu)^2, \\qquad y_j = \\frac{u_j - \\mu}{\\sqrt{\\sigma^2 + \\varepsilon}}\\, g_j + b_j$$\n\nTwo reductions per row (the mean, then the variance around it — the stable two-pass form from W-22), then an elementwise affine transform.\n\n**Bytes.** For 2048 rows of 1024 floats: read $x$ and $r$ (8 MiB each), write $y$ (8 MiB) = **24 MiB**. Unfused, $u$ would add a write and a read: 40 MiB. At 1792 GB/s that is the difference between 14 µs and 23 µs of pure traffic.\n\n## What you should see\n\nOne kernel, `k_ew_layer_norm_reduce`, 17.7 µs on the simulated RTX 5090, 24 MiB of DRAM traffic: the minimum. The schedule is one block of 1024 threads per row, with the row of $u$ staged in shared memory (`STAGE=all`). The three sweeps (mean, variance, normalize) read $u$ from shared memory, not DRAM.\n\nThe checks verify both the structure (\"one kernel\") and the maths: every output row has mean ≈ 0 and variance ≈ 1 (with $g = 1$, $b = 0$).\n\n## The system\n\n- The residual add is an elementwise producer read by three sweeps of the row. Inlining it means computing $x_j + r_j$ three times, which the fusion guard accepts: three adds are cheaper than a store and a load (W-09 explains the work estimate).\n- With `STAGE=all`, the kernel computes $u$ once while staging it, so even the recomputation disappears.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Return $x + r$ as a second output (transformers need the updated residual stream). What happens to the kernel count and the bytes? Is the result still optimal?\n2. Compare with RMSNorm (W-05) on the [A100](lab:device?id=sim:a100&measure): does the extra reduction cost time on a memory-bound kernel?\n3. Why does the model pick 1024 threads per row here but 128 for the softmax in W-06? (Hint: rows, SMs and waves.)\n"},{"id":"W-14","title":"Your own pass in the ML pipeline","group":"Extend the compiler","about":"a Tensor-IR rewrite (divide by a row value → multiply by its reciprocal), offered as a fork and inserted into the standard pipeline","tries":["Pin RECIP=false and diff the CUDA","Make the pass also handle division by a scalar constant"],"text":"/** W-14 Your own pass in the ML pipeline\n *  group  Extend the compiler\n *  about  a Tensor-IR rewrite (divide by a row value → multiply by its reciprocal), offered as a fork and inserted into the standard pipeline\n *  try    Pin RECIP=false and diff the CUDA | Make the pass also handle division by a scalar constant\n */\n// Softmax ends with e / Σe, where Σe is one value per row broadcast along the row. Division costs ~4× a\n// multiply, and the compiler does one per element. Rewrite it to e · (1/Σe): after fusion, loop-invariant\n// code motion computes 1/Σe once per row. The rewrite is a FORK (k.fork.toggle) with the knob RECIP, so\n// the original stays available: pin it in Knobs, or let Tune measure both.\nconst { tensor: Tn } = std.emmy.dialects;\nconst RECIP = param.bool(\"RECIP\", { off: false, help: \"divide by a broadcast value → multiply by its reciprocal\" });\nconst numel = (s) => s.reduce((a, b) => a * b, 1);\n\nconst hoistRecip = k.rule(\"hoist_reciprocal\")\n  .match(P.op(\"tensor.ew\").as(\"n\").where(({ n }) => n.attrs.fn === \"div\" && n.arg(1).is(Tn.view) && numel(n.arg(1).arg(0).shape) < numel(n.shape)))\n  .rewrite(({ n }) => k.fork.toggle(RECIP, () => {\n    const v = n.arg(1);                                     // the broadcast: view(Σe) to the row's shape\n    const r = T.ew(\"recip\", v.arg(0));                      // 1/Σe at its own (smaller) shape\n    return T.ew(\"mul\", n.arg(0), Tn.view(r, { shape: v.attrs.shape, map: v.attrs.map }));\n  }));\n\nconst recipPass = k.pass(\"frontend/reciprocal\").short(\"r\").rules(hoistRecip).knobs(RECIP)\n  .describe(\"x / broadcast(b) → x · broadcast(1/b)\")\n  .check((g) => !g.findAll(\"tensor.ew\").some((v) => v.attrs.fn === \"div\" && v.knobs.RECIP === true) || \"a div survived the rewrite\");\n\n// a cost for this fork (fewer flops win), in front of the standard policy chain\nconst flops = k.decide.cost(({ knobs }) => (knobs.RECIP ? 1 : 4), { name: \"flops\" }).for(\"hoist_reciprocal\");\nconst base = std.emmy.pipeline().insertAfter(\"frontend/optimization\", recipPass);\nconst pipe = base.with({ decide: k.decide.chain(k.decide.pinned(), flops, base._decide) });\n\nconst rows = param.int(\"rows\", { min: 16, max: 8192, default: 1024, scale: \"log\" });\nconst cols = param.int(\"cols\", { min: 32, max: 8192, default: 1024, scale: \"log\" });\nproject(\"softmax-recip\")\n  .pipeline(pipe)\n  .params(rows, cols)\n  .program(({ x }) => ({ y: N.softmax(x, -1) }), ({ param }) => ({ x: k.tensor([param(rows), param(cols)]) }))\n  .check(\"the reciprocal is computed once per row\", ({ run }) => {\n    if (!run.params.get(RECIP) && run.decisions.some((d) => d.knobs?.RECIP === false)) return true;\n    const body = run.at(\"loop\").findAll(\"loop.kernel\").map((v) => v.print()).join(\"\\n\");\n    return /recip/.test(body) || \"no recip in the Loop IR\";\n  });\n","notes":"# Your own pass in the ML pipeline\n\nA compiler pass you write, inserted into the standard ML pipeline. Softmax ends with $e_j / \\sum_k e_k$: a division per element by a value that is the same for the whole row. Division costs several multiplies, so the pass rewrites it to $e_j \\cdot (1/\\sum_k e_k)$, and code motion computes the reciprocal once per row.\n\n> **Try it** — the pass is a fork with the knob **RECIP**, listed under *knobs* in the Code view's Parameters panel (also in [Knobs](lab:view?name=knobs)). Pin it [off](lab:pin?RECIP=false) and [on](lab:pin?RECIP=true), and diff the [loop IR](lab:ir?name=loop) and the [CUDA](lab:view?name=kernels). [Unpin](lab:unpin?name=RECIP).\n\n## The rule\n\n```js\nconst hoistRecip = k.rule(\"hoist_reciprocal\")\n  .match(P.op(\"tensor.ew\").as(\"n\").where(({ n }) =>\n    n.attrs.fn === \"div\" && n.arg(1).is(Tn.view) && numel(n.arg(1).arg(0).shape) < numel(n.shape)))\n  .rewrite(({ n }) => k.fork.toggle(RECIP, () => { … T.ew(\"mul\", n.arg(0), view(recip(Σe))) … }));\n```\n\n- **The pattern** matches a division whose divisor is a *broadcast*: a `view` of a smaller tensor. Only then is there something to hoist.\n- **The rewrite** builds `recip` at the small shape and broadcasts the result. Because the reciprocal sits *before* the broadcast, loop-invariant code motion places it at the row level of the loop nest: once per row, not once per element.\n- **`k.fork.toggle(RECIP, …)`** makes it a choice, not a forced rewrite: the original and the rewritten graph are both options, and a decide policy picks.\n\n## The pass and its place\n\n```js\nconst recipPass = k.pass(\"frontend/reciprocal\").short(\"r\").rules(hoistRecip).knobs(RECIP)\n  .check((g) => …no div left with RECIP on… || \"a div survived the rewrite\");\nconst base = std.emmy.pipeline().insertAfter(\"frontend/optimization\", recipPass);\nconst flops = k.decide.cost(({ knobs }) => (knobs.RECIP ? 1 : 4), { name: \"flops\" }).for(\"hoist_reciprocal\");\n```\n\n- **`.check`** is a pass postcondition, run after the pass on every compile. Pass checks catch bugs where they happen instead of three IRs later.\n- **`insertAfter`** puts the pass after the tensor-level optimizations, so it sees the decomposed softmax.\n- **The policy** scores the fork by flops (a multiply 1, a division 4) and goes in front of the standard chain. In [Knobs](lab:view?name=knobs) the decision reads *by flops*.\n\n## Does it matter?\n\nThe softmax at 1024 × 1024 runs in 7.9 µs either way on the simulated RTX 5090: it is memory-bound (W-06), and the arithmetic hides under the DRAM traffic. On a real GPU, `div.rn.f32` is a multi-instruction sequence while `rcp` + `mul` is two, so the rewrite matters for compute-bound kernels. A fast-math compiler flag does the same globally, with less control. This is how to think about any rewrite: **it saves what the kernel is bound by, or it saves nothing.**\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Extend the pattern to division by a scalar constant ($x / 3 \\to x \\cdot 0.\\overline{3}$). Is that rewrite exact in fp32? (Hint: 3 is not a power of two.)\n2. Make RECIP's cost depend on the device: a policy that returns `null` (no opinion) on WebGPU.\n3. Write a pass check that fails if any `recip` sits inside a per-element loop in the Loop IR.\n"},{"id":"W-15","title":"Your own cost model","group":"Extend the compiler","about":"replace the schedule cost model with your own (occupancy first): it maps a matmul one row per block and runs 18× slower","tries":["Tune, then compare the measured best with what this model picked","Write a model that prefers fewer blocks"],"text":"/** W-15 Your own cost model\n *  group  Extend the compiler\n *  about  replace the schedule cost model with your own (occupancy first): it maps a matmul one row per block and runs 18× slower\n *  try    Tune, then compare the measured best with what this model picked | Write a model that prefers fewer blocks\n */\n// Every schedule fork is ranked by a decide policy. The default chain is: pins → measured evidence →\n// std.tile.costPrior (an analytic roofline + occupancy model) → first. Here the model is replaced by one\n// that only cares about occupancy, ties broken by threads per block. Knobs shows each option's score\n// under \"occupancy\"; the model column is the standard model, for comparison. It is often wrong:\n// occupancy is not speed. Measure and Tune to see by how much.\nconst { analyze, estimate } = std.tile;\nconst occupancyFirst = k.decide.cost(({ knobs, node, ctx }) => {\n  if (!node.is(\"loop.kernel\")) return null;\n  const an = analyze(node.attrs.expr, node.attrs.shape, node.args.map((a) => a.shape));\n  const e = estimate(an, knobs, ctx.target);\n  if (!isFinite(e.us)) return Infinity;                      // illegal on this device\n  return -e.occupancy + (knobs.TPB || knobs.BM * knobs.BN / ((knobs.TM || 1) * (knobs.TN || 1)) || 0) * 1e-6;\n}, { name: \"occupancy\" }).for(\"schedule\");\n\nconst pipe = std.emmy.pipeline().with({ decide: k.decide.chain(k.decide.pinned(), k.decide.evidence(), occupancyFirst, k.decide.first()) });\n\nconst rows = param.int(\"rows\", { min: 16, max: 8192, default: 2048, scale: \"log\" });\nconst dim = param.int(\"dim\", { min: 64, max: 8192, default: 1024, scale: \"log\" });\nproject(\"occupancy\")\n  .pipeline(pipe)\n  .params(rows, dim)\n  .program(({ x }, { param }) => {\n    const d = param(dim);\n    return { y: N.rms_norm(N.gelu(N.linear(x, std.weight(\"W\", [d, d]))), std.weight(\"w\", [d])) };\n  }, ({ param }) => ({ x: k.tensor([param(rows), param(dim)]) }))\n  // an info check: what the standard model thinks of each pick this policy made\n  .check(\"occupancy-first vs the standard model\", ({ run }) => {\n    const g = run.at(\"loop\"), out = [];\n    for (const d of run.decisions) {\n      const node = d.node && g.node(d.node) && g.value(d.node);\n      if (!node || !node.is(\"loop.kernel\")) continue;\n      const an = analyze(node.attrs.expr, node.attrs.shape, node.args.map((a) => a.shape));\n      const est = d.options.map((o) => estimate(an, o.knobs, run.ctx.target).us);\n      const mine = est[d.options.findIndex((o) => o.label === d.label)], best = Math.min(...est);\n      out.push(`${std.tile.canonRow(d.knobs)}: model says ${mine.toFixed(1)} µs, its own favourite ${best.toFixed(1)} µs (${(mine / best).toFixed(1)}×)`);\n    }\n    return { ok: true, message: out.join(\" · \") || \"no schedule forks\" };\n  });\n","notes":"# Your own cost model\n\nThe compiler chooses each kernel's schedule with a **decide policy**. This project swaps the standard analytic model for a tempting heuristic: *maximize occupancy*. On a matmul it picks one row per block with 64 threads, and runs 18× slower. A cost model must model the bottleneck, not a proxy for it.\n\n> **Try it** — [measure](lab:measure) and read the info check in the [Code view](lab:view?name=code). Then pin the standard model's choice for the matmul, [`MAP=tiled` 128×128](lab:pin?MAP=tiled&BM=128&BN=128&BK=8&TM=8&TN=2&measure), and [unpin](lab:unpin).\n\n## The policy\n\n```js\nconst occupancyFirst = k.decide.cost(({ knobs, node, ctx }) => {\n  const e = estimate(analyze(…node…), knobs, ctx.target);\n  if (!isFinite(e.us)) return Infinity;           // illegal on this device\n  return -e.occupancy + threadsPerBlock(knobs) * 1e-6;   // most warps per SM, then smaller blocks\n}, { name: \"occupancy\" }).for(\"schedule\");\nconst pipe = std.emmy.pipeline().with({ decide: k.decide.chain(k.decide.pinned(), k.decide.evidence(), occupancyFirst, k.decide.first()) });\n```\n\nA cost function returns a number per option (lower is better), `Infinity` for illegal, or `null` for \"no opinion\" — then the next policy in the chain decides.\n\n## What happens\n\nThe program is $y = \\operatorname{RMSNorm}(\\operatorname{GELU}(xW^\\top))$ with $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2048 \\times 1024}$. Two kernels, simulated RTX 5090:\n\n| kernel | occupancy-first picks | time | the standard model picks | time |\n|---|---|---:|---|---:|\n| matmul | `MAP=row, TPB=64` — occupancy 1.00 | **3212 µs**, 1.3 TFLOP/s | tiled 128×128, 8×2 per thread — occupancy 0.67 | 181 µs, 23.7 TFLOP/s |\n| GELU + RMSNorm | `MAP=row, TPB=64` | 12.6 µs | `MAP=row, TPB=1024, STAGE=all` | 12.6 µs |\n\n**Why the row map loses by 18×.** One block per output row computes 1024 dot products of length 1024, reading $W$ straight from L2 for every row: $2048 \\times 4\\ \\text{MiB} = 8\\ \\text{GiB}$ of L2 traffic. The tiled map reads each element of $W$ once per 128-row tile from L2 and then serves it from shared memory and registers (W-21). Occupancy is 100% in the first case and 67% in the second — and it doesn't matter, because neither kernel is waiting for DRAM latency. One is bound by L2 bandwidth, the other by shared-memory bandwidth.\n\n**Why the norm kernel ties.** Memory-bound row kernels need enough warps to keep bytes in flight (W-23); here both choices have enough, so occupancy-first happens to agree with the model.\n\n## The maths: what a cost model must predict\n\nThe standard model, `std.tile.estimate`, counts per option: flops, DRAM bytes, L2 bytes, shared-memory wavefronts (with exact bank conflicts for tiled kernels) and warp instructions. It turns each count into time at that unit's peak rate, takes the maximum (the bottleneck), and derates by achieved occupancy and wave quantization:\n\n$$t = \\sum_\\text{waves} \\max_{u \\in \\{\\text{dram, l2, smem, issue}\\}} \\frac{\\text{count}_u \\cdot \\text{share}}{\\text{rate}_u \\cdot \\text{eff}_u(\\text{occupancy})} + t_\\text{launch}$$\n\nOccupancy appears only as an efficiency factor. A model that optimizes the factor and ignores the counts optimizes the wrong thing.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Write a cost model that ranks by *estimated L2 bytes* only. Which kernels does it get right, and which wrong?\n2. Make occupancy-first return `null` for any kernel with a contraction (`an.contraction`), so the chain falls through to `k.decide.first()`. What does `first` pick? Now add `std.tile.costPrior` to the chain instead.\n3. [Tune for 16 programs](lab:tune?strategy=sweep&budget=16). The evidence policy is in the chain in front of your model: does the measured evidence overrule it? (It only does once your model's own favourite has been measured.)\n\n## Further reading\n\n- Williams, Waterman, Patterson, *Roofline* (2009)\n- Chen et al., *TVM* (OSDI 2018) and *Learning to Optimize Tensor Programs* (2018): analytic vs learned cost models\n"},{"id":"W-16","title":"A new frontend op","group":"Extend the compiler","about":"add an op to the frontend with reference semantics, teach the decomposition pass to lower it, and it fuses like a built-in","tries":["Add hard_sigmoid the same way","What does the strict tensor checkpoint say if you remove the decomposition?"],"text":"/** W-16 A new frontend op\n *  group  Extend the compiler\n *  about  add an op to the frontend with reference semantics, teach the decomposition pass to lower it, and it fuses like a built-in\n *  try    Add hard_sigmoid the same way | What does the strict tensor checkpoint say if you remove the decomposition?\n */\n// hard_swish(x) = x · min(max(x + 3, 0), 6) / 6 (MobileNetV3). The op gets an eval (the reference every\n// lower level is checked against), a type rule and a print form. A decomposition rule rewrites it into\n// Tensor IR; pipeline.insertRule adds the rule to the standard decomposition pass. From there on it is\n// ordinary tensor code: it lifts and fuses into the matmul's epilogue. (Put it BEFORE the linear and the\n// fusion guard keeps it apart: inlined into the matmul it would be recomputed once per output column.)\nconst Ext = k.dialect(\"ext\").describe(\"my frontend extensions\")\n  .op(\"hard_swish\", { args: [\"x\"], traits: [\"pure\"], infer: ({ x }) => x.type, print: \"hard_swish({x})\",\n    eval: ({ x }) => { const o = new Tensor(x.shape); for (let i = 0; i < o.data.length; i++) { const v = x.data[i]; o.data[i] = (v * Math.min(Math.max(v + 3, 0), 6)) / 6; } return o; } });\n\nconst lowerHardSwish = std.decompose(\"ext.hard_swish\", ({ x }) => T.mul(x, T.div(T.ew(\"min\", T.ew(\"max\", T.add(x, 3), 0), 6), 6)));\nconst pipe = std.emmy.pipeline().insertRule(\"frontend/decomposition\", lowerHardSwish)\n  .withIr(\"torch\", { dialects: [...std.emmy.pipeline().steps[0].opts.dialects, Ext] });\n\nconst tokens = param.int(\"tokens\", { min: 16, max: 8192, default: 1024, scale: \"log\" });\nconst dim = param.int(\"dim\", { min: 64, max: 8192, default: 1024, scale: \"log\" });\nproject(\"hard-swish\")\n  .pipeline(pipe)\n  .params(tokens, dim)\n  .program(({ x }, { param }) => ({ y: Ext.hard_swish(N.linear(x, std.weight(\"W\", [param(dim), param(dim)]))) }), ({ param }) => ({ x: k.tensor([param(tokens), param(dim)]) }))\n  .check(\"hard_swish fused into the matmul's epilogue\", ({ run }) => run.graph.findAll(\"gpu.kernel\").length === 1 || `${run.graph.findAll(\"gpu.kernel\").length} kernels`);\n","notes":"# A new frontend op\n\nAdding an operator to a compiler takes three things: its **meaning** (a reference implementation every lower level is checked against), its **type rule**, and a **lowering** into ops the compiler already handles. This project adds `hard_swish` from MobileNetV3. After decomposition it is ordinary Tensor IR and fuses into a matmul's epilogue like a built-in.\n\n> **Try it** — compile and look for `ext.hard_swish` in the [torch IR](lab:ir?name=torch), and for its pieces in the [tensor IR](lab:ir?name=tensor). [Measure](lab:measure): one kernel.\n\n## The maths\n\n$$\\operatorname{hard\\_swish}(x) = x \\cdot \\frac{\\min(\\max(x + 3, 0), 6)}{6}$$\n\nA piecewise-linear approximation of $\\operatorname{swish}(x) = x \\cdot \\sigma(x)$: zero for $x \\le -3$, the identity for $x \\ge 3$, and a parabola in between. It needs no exponential, which is why it was designed for mobile CPUs.\n\n## The three pieces\n\n```js\nconst Ext = k.dialect(\"ext\")\n  .op(\"hard_swish\", { args: [\"x\"], traits: [\"pure\"], infer: ({ x }) => x.type, print: \"hard_swish({x})\",\n    eval: ({ x }) => { …x·min(max(x+3,0),6)/6 per element… } });                       // meaning + type\nconst lowerHardSwish = std.decompose(\"ext.hard_swish\",\n  ({ x }) => T.mul(x, T.div(T.ew(\"min\", T.ew(\"max\", T.add(x, 3), 0), 6), 6)));        // lowering\nconst pipe = std.emmy.pipeline().insertRule(\"frontend/decomposition\", lowerHardSwish)\n  .withIr(\"torch\", { dialects: [...std.emmy.pipeline().steps[0].opts.dialects, Ext] });\n```\n\n- **`eval`** is the semantics. The end-to-end check runs the program through it, and compares with the Loop IR's evaluation. If the decomposition were wrong (say, `/ 3` for `/ 6`), the check would fail.\n- **`insertRule`** adds the decomposition to the standard pass rather than writing a new pass.\n- **`withIr(\"torch\", …)`** registers the dialect at the first checkpoint. Checkpoints are **strict**: if an op isn't allowed at a level, compilation stops with an error naming the op and the rule that declined to lower it.\n\n## What you should see\n\nOne kernel, `k_linear_ext_hard_swish_reduce`: a 1024 × 1024 × 1024 tiled matmul (128×64 tiles, 4×2 per thread) with hard_swish in its epilogue, 119.8 µs on the simulated RTX 5090. That's the same time as the plain linear layer of W-08: the activation costs nothing. It is applied to each output once, in registers, before the single store.\n\nPut the activation *before* the linear layer instead, and the fusion guard keeps it as a separate kernel: inlined into the matmul's operand loads, it would be recomputed once per output column (W-09).\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Remove the `insertRule` line. What does the strict tensor checkpoint report?\n2. Add `hard_sigmoid(x) = min(max(x + 3, 0), 6) / 6` the same way, then rewrite hard_swish in terms of it. Does the fused kernel change?\n3. Add a deliberate bug to the decomposition (`T.add(x, 2)`). Which check catches it, and with what error?\n"},{"id":"W-17","title":"Regex → NFA → DFA → JavaScript","group":"Other compilers","about":"not every compiler is about tensors: a regular-expression compiler with five IRs, whole-graph passes and a code generator","tries":["Change the pattern to (a","b)*abb and watch the DFA shrink when minimize is on","Add character classes like [a-c]"],"text":"/** W-17 Regex → NFA → DFA → JavaScript\n *  group  Other compilers\n *  about  not every compiler is about tensors: a regular-expression compiler with five IRs, whole-graph passes and a code generator\n *  try    Change the pattern to (a|b)*abb and watch the DFA shrink when minimize is on | Add character classes like [a-c]\n */\n// IRs:  regex (the parsed pattern: char, any, cat, alt, star, plus, opt)\n//   ─desugar→ core (plus and opt rewritten away)  ─thompson→ nfa  ─subset, minimize→ dfa  ─codegen→ js\n// Every level evaluates, so the end-to-end check runs the source matcher and the generated code on the\n// same strings. The pattern is a text parameter: edit it in the Code view's parameters.\nconst RX = { kind: \"regex\" };\nconst pattern = param.text(\"pattern\", { default: \"(ab|a)*b+.c?\", help: \"literals, ., |, *, +, ?, ( )\" });\nconst MIN = param.bool(\"minimize\", { default: true, help: \"merge equivalent DFA states (Moore's algorithm)\" });\n\n// ---- the source dialect: each node evaluates to a matcher (s, i) → set of end positions\nconst ends = (f) => f;\nconst Re = k.dialect(\"re\")\n  .op(\"char\", { attrs: { c: \"a\" }, infer: () => RX, print: \"'{c}'\", eval: ({ c }) => (s, i) => (s[i] === c ? [i + 1] : []) })\n  .op(\"any\", { infer: () => RX, print: \".\", eval: () => (s, i) => (i < s.length ? [i + 1] : []) })\n  .op(\"eps\", { infer: () => RX, print: \"ε\", eval: () => (s, i) => [i] })\n  .op(\"cat\", { args: [\"a\", \"b\"], infer: () => RX, print: \"{a}·{b}\", eval: ({ a, b }) => (s, i) => [...new Set(a(s, i).flatMap((j) => b(s, j)))] })\n  .op(\"alt\", { args: [\"a\", \"b\"], infer: () => RX, print: \"{a} | {b}\", eval: ({ a, b }) => (s, i) => [...new Set([...a(s, i), ...b(s, i)])] })\n  .op(\"star\", { args: [\"a\"], infer: () => RX, print: \"{a}*\", eval: ({ a }) => (s, i) => { const seen = new Set([i]), q = [i]; while (q.length) for (const j of a(s, q.pop())) if (!seen.has(j)) { seen.add(j); q.push(j); } return [...seen]; } })\n  .op(\"plus\", { args: [\"a\"], infer: () => RX, print: \"{a}+\", eval: ({ a }) => (s, i) => { const seen = new Set(), q = a(s, i).slice(); q.forEach((j) => seen.add(j)); while (q.length) for (const j of a(s, q.pop())) if (!seen.has(j)) { seen.add(j); q.push(j); } return [...seen]; } })\n  .op(\"opt\", { args: [\"a\"], infer: () => RX, print: \"{a}?\", eval: ({ a }) => (s, i) => [...new Set([i, ...a(s, i)])] })\n  .op(\"match\", { args: [\"rx\", \"s\"], infer: () => k.scalar(\"bool\"), print: \"match({rx}, {s})\", eval: ({ rx, s }) => (rx(s, 0).includes(s.length) ? 1 : 0) });\n\n// a recursive-descent parser that builds the graph (runs while tracing)\nfunction parse(src) {\n  let i = 0;\n  const peek = () => src[i], eat = (c) => { if (src[i] !== c) throw new Error(`expected '${c}' at ${i} in /${src}/`); i++; };\n  const alt = () => { let a = cat(); while (peek() === \"|\") { i++; a = Re.alt(a, cat()); } return a; };\n  const cat = () => { let a = null; while (i < src.length && peek() !== \"|\" && peek() !== \")\") { const b = post(); a = a ? Re.cat(a, b) : b; } return a || Re.eps(); };\n  const post = () => { let a = atom(); for (;;) { if (peek() === \"*\") { i++; a = Re.star(a); } else if (peek() === \"+\") { i++; a = Re.plus(a); } else if (peek() === \"?\") { i++; a = Re.opt(a); } else return a; } };\n  const atom = () => { const c = src[i++]; if (c === \"(\") { const a = alt(); eat(\")\"); return a; } if (c === \".\") return Re.any(); if (c === \"\\\\\") return Re.char({ c: src[i++] }); if (\"*+?|)\".includes(c)) throw new Error(`unexpected '${c}' at ${i - 1} in /${src}/`); return Re.char({ c }); };\n  const r = alt();\n  if (i < src.length) throw new Error(`unexpected '${src[i]}' at ${i} in /${src}/`);\n  return r;\n}\n\n// ---- desugar: plus and opt in terms of the core\nconst desugar = k.pass(\"desugar\").short(\"d\").rules(\n  k.rule(\"plus\").match(P.op(\"re.plus\").as(\"n\")).rewrite(({ n }) => Re.cat(n.arg(0), Re.star(n.arg(0)))),\n  k.rule(\"opt\").match(P.op(\"re.opt\").as(\"n\")).rewrite(({ n }) => Re.alt(n.arg(0), Re.eps())),\n);\n\n// ---- automata dialects\nconst OTHER = \"\\u0000\";\nconst nfaRun = ({ s, nfa }) => {\n  const close = (set) => { const q = [...set]; while (q.length) for (const t of nfa.eps[q.pop()] || []) if (!set.has(t)) { set.add(t); q.push(t); } return set; };\n  let cur = close(new Set([nfa.start]));\n  for (const ch0 of s) { const ch = nfa.alphabet.includes(ch0) ? ch0 : OTHER; const nxt = new Set(); for (const st of cur) for (const [c, t] of nfa.edges[st] || []) if (c === ch || c === \"any\") nxt.add(t); cur = close(nxt); }\n  return cur.has(nfa.accept) ? 1 : 0;\n};\nconst dfaRun = ({ s, dfa }) => { let st = 0; for (const ch0 of s) { const ch = dfa.alphabet.includes(ch0) ? ch0 : OTHER; st = dfa.table[st]?.[ch]; if (st == null) return 0; } return dfa.accept.includes(st) ? 1 : 0; };\nconst Auto = k.dialect(\"auto\")\n  .op(\"nfa\", { args: [\"s\"], attrs: { nfa: null }, infer: () => k.scalar(\"bool\"), eval: nfaRun,\n    print: ({ nfa }) => `nfa(s) · ${nfa.n} states, ${nfa.edges.flat().length} edges, ${nfa.eps.flat().length} ε-edges`,\n    body: ({ nfa }) => nfa.edges.map((es, q) => `${q === nfa.start ? \"→\" : \" \"}${q === nfa.accept ? \"((\" + q + \"))\" : \" \" + q + \" \"}  ${[...es.map(([c, t]) => `${c === OTHER ? \"other\" : c}→${t}`), ...(nfa.eps[q] || []).map((t) => `ε→${t}`)].join(\"  \")}`).join(\"\\n\") })\n  .op(\"dfa\", { args: [\"s\"], attrs: { dfa: null }, infer: () => k.scalar(\"bool\"), eval: dfaRun,\n    print: ({ dfa }) => `dfa(s) · ${dfa.table.length} states over {${dfa.alphabet.map((c) => (c === OTHER ? \"other\" : c)).join(\", \")}}`,\n    body: ({ dfa }) => dfa.table.map((row, q) => `${q === 0 ? \"→\" : \" \"}${dfa.accept.includes(q) ? \"((\" + q + \"))\" : \" \" + q + \" \"}  ${Object.entries(row).map(([c, t]) => `${c === OTHER ? \"other\" : c}→${t}`).join(\"  \")}`).join(\"\\n\") });\nconst Js = k.dialect(\"js\")\n  .op(\"fn\", { args: [\"s\"], attrs: { source: \"\" }, infer: () => k.scalar(\"bool\"), print: \"js(s) · generated matcher\", body: ({ source }) => source,\n    eval: ({ s, source }) => new Function(\"s\", source)(s) });\n\n// ---- Thompson's construction: a whole-graph pass from the AST under match() to an NFA\nconst thompson = k.pass(\"thompson\").short(\"t\").whole(({ graph, edit }) => {\n  const m = graph.find(\"re.match\");\n  const edges = [], eps = [];\n  let n = 0;\n  const st = () => { edges.push([]); eps.push([]); return n++; };\n  const alphabet = new Set();\n  const build = (v) => {                                    // returns [start, accept]\n    switch (v.opName) {\n      case \"char\": { const a = st(), b = st(); edges[a].push([v.attrs.c, b]); alphabet.add(v.attrs.c); return [a, b]; }\n      case \"any\": { const a = st(), b = st(); edges[a].push([\"any\", b]); return [a, b]; }\n      case \"eps\": { const a = st(), b = st(); eps[a].push(b); return [a, b]; }\n      case \"cat\": { const [a1, b1] = build(v.arg(0)), [a2, b2] = build(v.arg(1)); eps[b1].push(a2); return [a1, b2]; }\n      case \"alt\": { const a = st(), [a1, b1] = build(v.arg(0)), [a2, b2] = build(v.arg(1)), b = st(); eps[a].push(a1, a2); eps[b1].push(b); eps[b2].push(b); return [a, b]; }\n      case \"star\": { const a = st(), [a1, b1] = build(v.arg(0)), b = st(); eps[a].push(a1, b); eps[b1].push(a1, b); return [a, b]; }\n      default: throw new Error(`thompson: unexpected ${v.name}`);\n    }\n  };\n  const [start, accept] = build(m.arg(\"rx\"));\n  edit.replaceAll(edit.node(Auto.nfa, [m.arg(\"s\")], { nfa: { n, edges, eps, start, accept, alphabet: [...alphabet, OTHER] } }));\n});\n\n// ---- subset construction, then (optionally) minimization\nconst subset = k.pass(\"subset\").short(\"s\").whole(({ graph, edit }) => {\n  const v = graph.find(\"auto.nfa\"), nfa = v.attrs.nfa;\n  const close = (set) => { const q = [...set]; while (q.length) for (const t of nfa.eps[q.pop()]) if (!set.has(t)) { set.add(t); q.push(t); } return [...set].sort((a, b) => a - b); };\n  const key = (xs) => xs.join(\",\"), start = close(new Set([nfa.start]));\n  const ids = new Map([[key(start), 0]]), sets = [start], table = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < sets.length; i++) {\n    table[i] = {};\n    for (const c of nfa.alphabet) {\n      const nxt = new Set();\n      for (const q of sets[i]) for (const [ec, t] of nfa.edges[q]) if (ec === c || ec === \"any\") nxt.add(t);\n      if (!nxt.size) continue;\n      const cl = close(nxt), kk = key(cl);\n      if (!ids.has(kk)) { ids.set(kk, sets.length); sets.push(cl); }\n      table[i][c] = ids.get(kk);\n    }\n  }\n  const accept = sets.map((s, i) => (s.includes(nfa.accept) ? i : -1)).filter((i) => i >= 0);\n  edit.replaceAll(edit.node(Auto.dfa, [v.arg(0)], { dfa: { table, accept, alphabet: nfa.alphabet } }));\n});\nconst minimize = k.pass(\"minimize\").short(\"m\").knobs(MIN).whole(({ graph, edit, param }) => {\n  if (!param(MIN)) return;\n  const v = graph.find(\"auto.dfa\"), { table, accept, alphabet } = v.attrs.dfa;\n  const D = table.length + 1;                                 // state D-1 is the dead state\n  const next = (q, c) => (q === D - 1 ? D - 1 : table[q][c] ?? D - 1);\n  let cls = Array.from({ length: D }, (_, q) => (accept.includes(q) ? 1 : 0));\n  for (;;) {                                                  // Moore: refine by (class, classes of successors)\n    const sig = cls.map((c, q) => c + \"|\" + alphabet.map((a) => cls[next(q, a)]).join(\",\"));\n    const ids = new Map(), nc = sig.map((s) => (ids.has(s) ? ids.get(s) : (ids.set(s, ids.size), ids.size - 1)));\n    if (ids.size === new Set(cls).size) break;\n    cls = nc;\n  }\n  // renumber so the start is 0, drop the dead class\n  const order = [cls[0]], dead = cls[D - 1];\n  for (let q = 0; q < D; q++) if (!order.includes(cls[q]) && cls[q] !== dead) order.push(cls[q]);\n  const id = new Map(order.map((c, i) => [c, i]));\n  const t2 = order.map(() => ({}));\n  for (let q = 0; q < D - 1; q++) for (const a of alphabet) { const r = next(q, a); if (cls[r] !== dead && id.has(cls[q])) t2[id.get(cls[q])][a] = id.get(cls[r]); }\n  const acc2 = [...new Set(accept.map((q) => id.get(cls[q])))];\n  edit.replaceAll(edit.node(Auto.dfa, [v.arg(0)], { dfa: { table: t2, accept: acc2, alphabet } }));\n});\n\n// ---- code generation: a switch-based matcher\nconst codegen = k.pass(\"codegen\").short(\"c\").whole(({ graph, edit }) => {\n  const v = graph.find(\"auto.dfa\"), { table, accept, alphabet } = v.attrs.dfa;\n  const lit = (c) => JSON.stringify(c);\n  const lines = [\"let q = 0;\", \"for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {\", `  const c = ${JSON.stringify(alphabet.filter((c) => c !== OTHER).join(\"\"))}.includes(s[i]) ? s[i] : ${lit(OTHER)};`, \"  switch (q) {\"];\n  table.forEach((row, q) => {\n    const arms = Object.entries(row).map(([c, t]) => `c === ${lit(c)} ? ${t}`);\n    lines.push(`    case ${q}: q = ${arms.length ? arms.join(\" : \") + \" : -1\" : \"-1\"}; break;`);\n  });\n  lines.push(\"  }\", \"  if (q < 0) return 0;\", \"}\", `return ${accept.length ? accept.map((a) => `q === ${a}`).join(\" || \") : \"false\"} ? 1 : 0;`);\n  edit.replaceAll(edit.node(Js.fn, [v.arg(0)], { source: lines.join(\"\\n\") }));\n});\n\nconst pipe = k.pipeline(\"regex\")\n  .params(pattern)\n  .ir(\"regex\", { dialects: [Re] })\n  .pass(desugar)\n  .ir(\"core\", { dialects: [Re], strict: true })\n  .pass(thompson)\n  .ir(\"nfa\", { dialects: [Auto], strict: true })\n  .pass(subset).pass(minimize)\n  .ir(\"dfa\", { dialects: [Auto], strict: true })\n  .pass(codegen)\n  .ir(\"js\", { dialects: [Js], strict: true })\n  .build();\n\nconst TESTS = [\"\", \"b\", \"ab\", \"abb\", \"bc\", \"abc\", \"aabb\", \"ababbbc\", \"abx\", \"ac\", \"babbab\", \"xb\", \"abab\", \"aaaaaaaaaab\", \"bbbbbbbbz\"];\nproject(\"regex\")\n  .pipeline(pipe)\n  .program(({ s }, { param }) => ({ m: Re.match(parse(param(pattern)), s) }), { s: { kind: \"string\" } })\n  .data({ s: \"ababbbc\" })\n  .check(\"generated code agrees with the regex on every test string\", ({ run, graph }) => {\n    const bad = TESTS.filter((s) => run.graph.eval({ s }).m !== graph.eval({ s }).m);\n    return !bad.length || `disagrees on ${bad.map((s) => JSON.stringify(s)).join(\", \")}`;\n  })\n  .check(\"agrees with JavaScript's RegExp\", ({ graph, run }) => {\n    const re = new RegExp(\"^(?:\" + run.params.get(pattern) + \")$\", \"s\");\n    const bad = TESTS.filter((s) => (re.test(s) ? 1 : 0) !== graph.eval({ s }).m);\n    return !bad.length || `disagrees on ${bad.map((s) => JSON.stringify(s)).join(\", \")}`;\n  });\n","notes":"# Regex → NFA → DFA → JavaScript\n\nCompilers are not only about tensors. This project uses the same framework — dialects, rewrite rules, whole-graph passes, IR checkpoints, an end-to-end check — for a classic: compiling a regular expression into a deterministic automaton and then into JavaScript.\n\n> **Try it** — edit the **pattern** in the Code view's Parameters panel, or try [`(a|b)*abb`](lab:param?name=pattern&value=%28a%7Cb%29*abb), the textbook example. Toggle [minimize off](lab:param?name=minimize&value=false) and [on](lab:param?name=minimize&value=true), and compare the [DFA](lab:ir?name=dfa). Read the generated matcher in the [js IR](lab:ir?name=js).\n\n## The pipeline\n\n| IR | what it holds | pass to the next level |\n|---|---|---|\n| **regex** | the parsed pattern: `char`, `any`, `cat`, `alt`, `star`, `plus`, `opt` | `desugar`: $a^+ \\to a\\,a^*$, $a? \\to a \\mid \\varepsilon$ (rewrite rules) |\n| **core** | only `char`, `any`, `eps`, `cat`, `alt`, `star` | `thompson`: a whole-graph pass building an NFA |\n| **nfa** | one node holding states, edges and ε-edges | `subset`, then `minimize` |\n| **dfa** | one node holding a transition table | `codegen` |\n| **js** | the source of a `switch`-based matcher | — |\n\nEvery level evaluates. The end-to-end check runs the source matcher and the generated function on 15 test strings, and a second check compares with JavaScript's own `RegExp`.\n\n## The maths\n\n**Thompson's construction** builds an NFA with ε-moves, compositionally: each operator becomes a small gadget wired to its operands' gadgets. Each operator adds at most two states, so the NFA has $O(m)$ states for a pattern of length $m$. The default pattern `(ab|a)*b+.c?` gives 24 states.\n\n**Subset construction** makes it deterministic: each DFA state is the set of NFA states the input could have reached, closed under ε-moves. In the worst case that is $2^{O(m)}$ states — `(a|b)*a(a|b)^{k}` needs $2^{k+1}$ — but typical patterns stay small.\n\n**Minimization (Moore's algorithm)** merges equivalent states. Start with two classes, accepting and not; repeatedly split a class whenever two of its states go, on some symbol, to different classes; stop when nothing splits. The result is the unique smallest DFA for the language.\n\n| pattern | NFA states | DFA states | minimized |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| `(ab\\|a)*b+.c?` | 24 | 10 | 8 |\n| `(a\\|b)*abb` | 14 | 5 | 4 |\n\nFor `(a|b)*abb` the minimized DFA is the one in the Dragon Book (states 0–3, accepting 3), and the generated code is a four-case `switch`.\n\n## The system\n\n- **Whole-graph passes** (`k.pass(…).whole(({ graph, edit }) => …)`) replace a subgraph in one step. Rewrite rules suit local patterns; algorithms like subset construction need the whole automaton.\n- **Strict checkpoints** guarantee each level only contains its own dialect's ops: after `thompson` no `re.*` op may survive.\n- **The pattern is a text parameter** and `minimize` a pipeline parameter. Changing either recompiles, and the checks run again.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Add character classes `[a-c]`: parse them as an alternation, or (better) add an edge label for a set and teach `subset` to split the alphabet into disjoint classes.\n2. Write a pattern whose DFA has many more states than its NFA. How many does `(a|b)*a(a|b)(a|b)(a|b)` have?\n3. Make the code generator emit a lookup table (`Uint8Array`) instead of a `switch`. Which is faster for long strings?\n\n## Further reading\n\n- Russ Cox, [*Regular Expression Matching Can Be Simple And Fast*](https://swtch.com/~rsc/regexp/regexp1.html)\n- Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman, *Compilers: Principles, Techniques, and Tools*, chapter 3\n"},{"id":"W-18","title":"The roofline: three regimes","group":"GPU optimization","about":"one elementwise kernel and two sliders: work per element moves it from memory-bound to compute-bound; size moves it to launch-bound","tries":["At what degree does this kernel become compute-bound on the A100, and why is that lower than on the RTX 5090?","Explain the time at 4096 elements"],"text":"/** W-18 The roofline: three regimes\n *  group  GPU optimization\n *  about  one elementwise kernel and two sliders: work per element moves it from memory-bound to compute-bound; size moves it to launch-bound\n *  try    At what degree does this kernel become compute-bound on the A100, and why is that lower than on the RTX 5090? | Explain the time at 4096 elements\n */\n// y = p(x), a polynomial of degree d evaluated per element in Horner form:\n//   y = ((…((c_d·x + c_{d−1})·x + c_{d−2})·x …)·x + c_0\n// Each degree costs one multiply and one add (2 flops); every element costs 8 bytes of DRAM traffic\n// (read x, write y) whatever the degree. So the arithmetic intensity is d/4 flop per byte: the degree\n// slider walks the kernel along the roofline's x axis. See the Notes.\nconst n = param.int(\"elements\", { min: 1024, max: 16777216, default: 4194304, scale: \"log\", help: \"tensor size\" });\nconst degree = param.int(\"degree\", { min: 1, max: 256, default: 8, scale: \"log\", help: \"polynomial degree: 2·degree flops per element\" });\n\nproject(\"roofline\")\n  .params(n, degree)\n  .program(({ x }, { param }) => {\n    const d = param(degree);\n    const c = (i) => 1 / (i + 1);                     // coefficients stay small: p(x) is bounded on [−1, 1)\n    let y = N.add(N.mul(x, c(d)), c(d - 1));\n    for (let i = d - 2; i >= 0; i--) y = N.add(N.mul(y, x), c(i));\n    return { y };\n  }, ({ param }) => ({ x: k.tensor([param(n)]) }))\n  .check(\"one kernel, 2·degree flops per element\", ({ run }) => {\n    const ks = run.graph.findAll(\"gpu.kernel\");\n    return ks.length === 1 || `${ks.length} kernels`;\n  });\n","notes":"# The roofline: three regimes\n\nEvery kernel is limited by one of three things: **arithmetic** (compute-bound), **moving bytes** (memory-bound), or **fixed overhead** (launch-bound). This project is one elementwise kernel with two sliders. Moving them carries the kernel through all three regimes, and the roofline model predicts where it ends up.\n\n> **Try it** — [measure now](lab:measure), then [degree 256](lab:param?name=degree&value=256&measure) for compute-bound and [4096 elements](lab:param?name=elements&value=4096&measure) for launch-bound. [Reset](lab:param?name=elements&value=4194304) before the next section.\n\n## The program\n\nFor every element $x_i$ the kernel evaluates a polynomial of degree $d$ in Horner form:\n\n$$y_i = \\Big(\\big((c_d\\,x_i + c_{d-1})\\,x_i + c_{d-2}\\big)\\,x_i + \\dots\\Big)\\,x_i + c_0$$\n\nEach Horner step is one multiply and one add, which the `frontend/optimization` pass contracts into one **fused multiply-add** (`fma`, the `contract_fma` rule — see it in [Trace](lab:view?name=trace)). An FMA counts as 2 floating-point operations but issues as 1 instruction. So per element:\n\n| quantity | per element | for $n$ elements |\n|---|---|---|\n| work $W$ | $2d$ flop | $2dn$ |\n| DRAM traffic $Q$ | 4 B read + 4 B write | $8n$ bytes |\n| arithmetic intensity $I = W/Q$ | | $d/4$ flop/byte |\n\nThe degree changes the work but not the bytes. **Intensity is the x-axis of the roofline**, and the degree slider walks along it.\n\n## The maths: the roofline\n\nA GPU has a peak arithmetic rate $\\pi$ (flop/s) and a peak memory bandwidth $\\beta$ (bytes/s). A kernel with intensity $I$ cannot go faster than either:\n\n$$P(I) \\;=\\; \\min\\big(\\pi,\\; \\beta \\cdot I\\big)$$\n\nThe two lines meet at the **ridge point** $I^* = \\pi/\\beta$. Below it, more arithmetic per byte is free: the memory system is the bottleneck. Above it, every extra flop costs time.\n\nTurned around, the best possible time of a kernel is\n\n$$t \\;\\ge\\; t_\\text{launch} + \\max\\!\\left(\\frac{Q}{\\beta},\\; \\frac{W}{\\pi}\\right)$$\n\nand the term that wins names the regime. For this kernel the crossover degree is $d^* = 4 I^*$:\n\n| GPU (simulated, FP32) | $\\pi$ | $\\beta$ | ridge $I^*$ | crossover degree $d^*$ |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| RTX 5090 | 104.8 TFLOP/s | 1792 GB/s | 58.5 | 234 |\n| RTX 4090 | 82.6 | 1008 | 81.9 | 328 |\n| H100 SXM | 67 | 3350 | 20.0 | 80 |\n| A100 80GB | 19.5 | 2039 | 9.6 | 38 |\n| Orin Nano class | 1.28 | 68 | 18.8 | 75 |\n\nFP32 throughput counts the CUDA cores only. Tensor cores, which this compiler does not use, move $\\pi$ up by 10× or more on the datacenter parts. That is why \"memory-bound\" is the normal state of affairs for everything except big matmuls.\n\n### Worked example: 4,194,304 elements on the RTX 5090\n\n- $Q = 8 \\times 4{,}194{,}304 = 33.6$ MB, so $Q/\\beta = 33.6\\text{ MB} / 1792\\text{ GB/s} = 18.7\\ \\mu s$.\n- At $d = 8$: $W = 67$ Mflop, $W/\\pi = 0.64\\ \\mu s$. Memory wins by 30×.\n- At $d = 256$: $W = 2.15$ Gflop, $W/\\pi = 20.5\\ \\mu s$. Now compute wins, narrowly.\n- Add the launch overhead the simulator assumes, $t_\\text{launch} = 2.5\\ \\mu s$: the bound is about $21.2\\ \\mu s$ at low degree and $23\\ \\mu s$ at $d = 256$.\n\n## What you should see\n\nSimulated, 4,194,304 elements:\n\n| degree | intensity | RTX 5090 | A100 | H100 |\n|---:|---:|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 0.25 | 21.4 µs, dram | 19.0 µs, dram | 12.5 µs, dram |\n| 8 | 2 | 21.4 µs, dram | 19.0 µs, dram | 12.5 µs, dram |\n| 32 | 8 | 21.4 µs, dram | 19.0 µs, dram | 12.5 µs, dram |\n| 64 | 16 | 21.7 µs, dram | **30.1 µs, compute** | 12.6 µs, dram |\n| 128 | 32 | 21.4 µs, dram | 57.6 µs, compute | **19.1 µs, issue** |\n| 256 | 64 | **23.2 µs, issue** | 113.2 µs, compute | 35.3 µs, issue |\n\nFrom degree 1 to 32 the time does not move at all: 32× more arithmetic for free. That flat line is the memory roof. Each GPU leaves it near its own $d^*$: the A100 first (its FP32 peak is low next to its HBM bandwidth), then the H100, and the 5090 only at the top of the slider.\n\n\"issue\" means the scheduler's instruction rate. One FMA is one instruction, so issue-bound and compute-bound are the same wall here; the few load, store and index instructions on top tip it over.\n\nNow the size, at degree 8 on the RTX 5090:\n\n| elements | time | bound | achieved bandwidth |\n|---:|---:|---|---:|\n| 1,024 | 2.7 µs | launch | 3 GB/s |\n| 16,384 | 2.8 µs | launch | 47 GB/s |\n| 262,144 | 3.8 µs | dram | 557 GB/s |\n| 1,048,576 | 7.3 µs | dram | 1142 GB/s |\n| 16,777,216 | 77.6 µs | dram | 1729 GB/s |\n\nBelow a few hundred thousand elements the kernel is **overhead-bound**. The GPU finishes the work in nanoseconds and the 2.5 µs launch is the whole bill. Achieved bandwidth only approaches the peak once the transfer is long enough to amortize it.\n\n## The system: where these numbers come from\n\n- **Compile.** `N.add(N.mul(y, x), c)` → the `nn` dialect → decomposition into `tensor.ew` ops → `contract_fma` → lifted into one `loop.kernel` by fusion. See the [loop IR](lab:ir?name=loop): one load of `x`, $d$ FMAs, one store.\n- **Schedule.** The tile pass forks over thread-per-element schedules (`TPB`, `COARSEN`). The cost model ranks them; see [Knobs](lab:view?name=knobs).\n- **Measure.** The simulated GPU executes sampled blocks of the generated kernel lockstep, warp by warp. It counts flops, warp instructions and DRAM sectors, then converts the counts to time with the roofline-plus-occupancy model above. The roofline in [Perf](lab:view?name=perf) plots the point.\n\n> **Caveat** — The simulator turns counts into time with a model, so these are not hardware numbers. Real GPUs add caches, clocks that boost and throttle, and launch overheads that vary. The shape — flat, then rising; a floor at small sizes — is what transfers. Try [WebGPU](lab:device?id=webgpu&measure) on your own GPU.\n\n## Takeaways\n\n1. Compute the intensity before optimizing. If $I \\ll I^*$, cutting flops does nothing; only cutting bytes helps.\n2. Elementwise ops have $I < 1$: they are always memory-bound, which is why **fusion** (next: W-19) is the main optimization for them.\n3. Small kernels are overhead-bound: batch them, fuse them, or capture them in a CUDA graph.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Make the polynomial read a second input (a per-element coefficient tensor). What happens to $Q$, $I$ and $d^*$?\n2. Switch to the [A100](lab:device?id=sim:a100&measure) and find the smallest degree at which the bound changes. Compare with $d^* = 38$.\n3. Pin `COARSEN=4` in Knobs at 16M elements: does processing 4 elements per thread change anything for a memory-bound kernel? Why not?\n\n## Further reading\n\n- Williams, Waterman, Patterson, *Roofline: An Insightful Visual Performance Model* (CACM 2009)\n- Horace He, [Making Deep Learning Go Brrrr From First Principles](https://horace.io/brrr_intro.html): compute, memory, overhead\n- [Modal GPU Glossary](https://modal.com/gpu-glossary): arithmetic intensity, memory-bound, ridge point\n"},{"id":"W-19","title":"Fusion: why the compiler fuses","group":"GPU optimization","about":"a transformer epilogue (bias, GELU, scale, residual) with fusion on and off: count the kernels, the DRAM bytes and the microseconds","tries":["Turn fusion off and explain where the extra time goes, kernel by kernel","How many bytes per element does each version move?"],"text":"/** W-19 Fusion: why the compiler fuses\n *  group  GPU optimization\n *  about  a transformer epilogue (bias, GELU, scale, residual) with fusion on and off: count the kernels, the DRAM bytes and the microseconds\n *  try    Turn fusion off and explain where the extra time goes, kernel by kernel | How many bytes per element does each version move?\n */\n// y = r + s · GELU(x + b): the elementwise tail of a transformer block. Every op reads its inputs from DRAM\n// and writes its output back, unless the compiler fuses: then x, b and r are read once, y is written once,\n// and every intermediate lives in registers. The FUSE knob switches the fusion pass's rule on and off;\n// nothing else changes. See the Notes for the byte counting.\nconst FUSE = param.bool(\"fuse\", { default: true, help: \"run loop fusion (inline producers into consumers)\" });\nconst tokens = param.int(\"tokens\", { min: 256, max: 16384, default: 4096, scale: \"log\" });\nconst hidden = param.int(\"hidden\", { min: 256, max: 8192, default: 1024, scale: \"log\" });\n\n// Wrap the standard fusion rule: same pattern, same guard, but it declines (visibly, in Trace) when FUSE is off.\nconst merge = std.loop.mergeLoopOps;\nconst maybeMerge = k.rule(\"merge_loop_ops (switchable)\").match(merge.pat)\n  .mutate((ctx) => { if (!ctx.param(FUSE)) ctx.skip(\"fusion is switched off\"); return merge.mut(ctx); });\nfor (const g of merge.guards) maybeMerge.guard(g);\nconst fusion = k.pass(\"loop/fusion\").short(\"f\").order(\"reverse\").rules(maybeMerge).describe(\"fuse loop kernels, unless FUSE is off\");\n\nproject(\"fusion\")\n  .pipeline(std.emmy.pipeline().replace(\"loop/fusion\", fusion).with({ name: \"emmy (switchable fusion)\" }))\n  .params(FUSE, tokens, hidden)\n  .program(({ x, r }, { param }) => {\n    const b = std.weight(\"bias\", [param(hidden)]);\n    return { y: N.add(r, N.mul(N.gelu(N.add(x, b)), 0.5)) };\n  }, ({ param }) => { const t = k.tensor([param(tokens), param(hidden)]); return { x: t, r: t }; })\n  .check(\"kernels\", ({ run }) => {\n    const ks = run.graph.findAll(\"gpu.kernel\").length;\n    return { ok: run.params.get(FUSE) ? ks === 1 : ks > 1, message: `${ks} kernel${ks === 1 ? \"\" : \"s\"} with fusion ${run.params.get(FUSE) ? \"on\" : \"off\"}` };\n  });\n","notes":"# Fusion: why the compiler fuses\n\nThe elementwise tail of a transformer block — add a bias, apply GELU, scale, add the residual — does almost no arithmetic. Its cost is entirely **bytes**. Fusion turns eleven passes over memory into one. This project has a `fuse` switch so you can measure the difference.\n\n> **Try it** — [measure fused](lab:measure), then [turn fusion off](lab:param?name=fuse&value=false&measure) and compare in [Perf](lab:view?name=perf). [Turn it back on](lab:param?name=fuse&value=true) when you're done.\n\n## The program\n\n$$y = r + s \\cdot \\operatorname{GELU}(x + b), \\qquad \\operatorname{GELU}(u) = \\tfrac12\\,u\\,\\Big(1 + \\tanh\\!\\big(\\sqrt{2/\\pi}\\,(u + 0.044715\\,u^3)\\big)\\Big)$$\n\n$x$ and $r$ are `tokens × hidden` (4096 × 1024 by default); $b$ is a `hidden` vector broadcast over the rows; $s = 0.5$.\n\nThe `frontend/decomposition` pass rewrites `GELU` into its primitive operations. Together with the bias add, the scale and the residual add, that is about a dozen elementwise operations; see the [tensor IR](lab:ir?name=tensor).\n\n## The maths: counting bytes\n\nLet $n$ = tokens × hidden = 4,194,304 elements, 4 bytes each, so one tensor is $4n$ bytes = 16 MiB.\n\n**Unfused**, every operation is its own kernel. Each one reads its inputs from DRAM and writes its result back:\n\n$$Q_\\text{unfused} = \\sum_{k \\in \\text{kernels}} 4n\\,\\big(\\text{inputs}_k + 1\\big)$$\n\n**Fused**, there is one kernel. It reads $x$ and $r$, reads $b$ once per row (4 KiB, which stays in cache), and writes $y$:\n\n$$Q_\\text{fused} \\approx 3 \\cdot 4n = 12n \\text{ bytes} = 48\\text{ MiB}$$\n\nEvery intermediate lives in a register for a few cycles and never touches memory.\n\nBoth versions do the same arithmetic, about 19 flop per element, so the intensity is $19/12 \\approx 1.6$ flop/byte fused and roughly ten times lower unfused. That is far below the ridge point of any GPU (W-18), so\n\n$$t \\approx \\frac{Q}{\\beta} + K \\cdot t_\\text{launch}$$\n\nwhere $K$ is the number of kernels.\n\n## What you should see\n\nSimulated, RTX 5090 ($\\beta$ = 1792 GB/s, $t_\\text{launch}$ = 2.5 µs):\n\n| | kernels | DRAM traffic | predicted $Q/\\beta + K t_\\text{launch}$ | simulated |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| fused | 1 | 48 MiB | 28.1 + 2.5 = 30.6 µs | 30.9 µs |\n| unfused | 11 | 416 MiB | 243 + 27.5 = 271 µs | 273.8 µs |\n\nThat is a **8.9× speedup from moving fewer bytes**, with the same arithmetic. The unfused version even pays 16 MiB to materialize the bias broadcast: `k_ew` writes a full `tokens × hidden` copy of $b$, because without fusion a broadcast view becomes a real tensor.\n\nLook at the unfused [loop IR](lab:ir?name=loop): eleven `loop.kernel` nodes, each a one-line loop nest. Fused, there is one kernel whose body is the whole expression; the [Graph](lab:graph?name=loop) shows the difference at a glance.\n\n## The system: how this compiler fuses\n\n1. **Lift.** `loop/lowering` turns each tensor op into a `loop.kernel`: an expression over output coordinates, with loads from its inputs.\n2. **Fuse.** `loop/fusion` repeatedly inlines a producer kernel's expression into its consumer, so the consumer recomputes the producer's value where it used to load it. The pass scans **consumers first** (`order(\"reverse\")`).\n3. **Guard.** Inlining duplicates the producer's work at every use. A guard compares the work before and after, where a memory access costs 12 work units, and declines when the work would blow up. That is why the matmuls in W-09 stay separate.\n4. **Switch.** This project wraps the standard rule: `maybeMerge` uses the same pattern and the same guards, but calls `skip(\"fusion is switched off\")` when `fuse` is false. Turn on *Declined* in [Trace](lab:view?name=trace) to see it decline 10 times.\n\nReductions fuse too. A reduction's consumers are inlined, and loop-invariant code motion places each value at the deepest loop it depends on. That is how RMSNorm (W-05) and softmax (W-06) become one kernel each.\n\n## When fusion is the wrong call\n\n- **Recomputation.** Inlining a matmul into another matmul recomputes it per output element. The guard declines it (W-09).\n- **Register pressure.** A fused kernel holds more live values per thread. Past the register budget, occupancy drops (W-23) or values spill to local memory.\n- **Different parallel shapes.** A row reduction feeding an elementwise op over a different axis may need different thread mappings; forcing them into one kernel can serialize work.\n\n## Takeaways\n\n1. For memory-bound ops, **time ≈ bytes / bandwidth**. Count the bytes and you can predict the speedup before running anything.\n2. Fusion is the single biggest optimization for the \"glue\" of neural networks: norms, activations, residuals, dropout, bias. This is the core of what `torch.compile`, XLA and TVM do.\n3. Launch overhead adds up: 11 kernels cost 27.5 µs before moving a byte.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Predict, then measure: what does the unfused version cost at `hidden = 256`? Which term dominates now?\n2. Add dropout-style masking, `N.mul(y, N.gt(x, 0))`, to the program. How many extra bytes does it cost fused? Unfused?\n3. Write a rule that fuses only elementwise producers, never reductions. Is anything in this program affected?\n\n## Further reading\n\n- Horace He, [Making Deep Learning Go Brrrr](https://horace.io/brrr_intro.html), the section on operator fusion\n- [MLC course](https://mlc.ai/summer22/), the lectures on graph-level optimization\n- TVM, [*An Automated End-to-End Optimizing Compiler for Deep Learning*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.04799), section 3 on operator fusion\n"},{"id":"W-20","title":"Batch size and arithmetic intensity","group":"GPU optimization","about":"one linear layer, from batch 1 (LLM decoding: memory-bound) to batch 1024 (prefill: compute-bound); the weights are the bytes","tries":["Why does batch 8 cost almost the same as batch 1?","At what batch does this layer stop being memory-bound on the H100?"],"text":"/** W-20 Batch size and arithmetic intensity\n *  group  GPU optimization\n *  about  one linear layer, from batch 1 (LLM decoding: memory-bound) to batch 1024 (prefill: compute-bound); the weights are the bytes\n *  try    Why does batch 8 cost almost the same as batch 1? | At what batch does this layer stop being memory-bound on the H100?\n */\n// y = x · Wᵀ with W of shape (d, d). The weights are read once per call whatever the batch, so the bytes\n// barely change with the batch M while the flops grow linearly: intensity ≈ M/2 flop per byte for small M.\n// Decoding one token at a time (M = number of concurrent sequences) sits far left on the roofline;\n// prefill (M = prompt length) sits far right. See the Notes for the derivation.\nconst M = param.int(\"batch\", { min: 1, max: 1024, default: 1, scale: \"log\", help: \"rows of x: tokens processed together\" });\nconst d = param.int(\"d_model\", { min: 512, max: 4096, default: 2048, scale: \"log\", help: \"W is d×d\" });\n\nproject(\"batch\")\n  .params(M, d)\n  .program(({ x }, { param }) => ({ y: N.linear(x, std.weight(\"W\", [param(d), param(d)])) }), ({ param }) => ({ x: k.tensor([param(M), param(d)]) }))\n  .check(\"arithmetic intensity\", ({ run }) => {\n    const m = run.params.get(M), n = run.params.get(d);\n    const flops = 2 * m * n * n, bytes = 4 * (m * n + n * n + m * n);\n    return { ok: true, message: `${(flops / 1e6).toFixed(1)} MFLOP over ${(bytes / 1048576).toFixed(1)} MiB: ${(flops / bytes).toFixed(2)} flop/byte (≈ M/2 = ${(m / 2).toFixed(1)} for small M)` };\n  });\n","notes":"# Batch size and arithmetic intensity\n\nWhy is generating text one token at a time so slow, while processing a long prompt is fast? Why does serving many users at once barely cost more than serving one? All three answers come from one formula: the **arithmetic intensity of a matrix–vector versus a matrix–matrix product**.\n\n> **Try it** — [measure at batch 1](lab:measure), then [batch 8](lab:param?name=batch&value=8&measure), [batch 128](lab:param?name=batch&value=128&measure) and [batch 1024](lab:param?name=batch&value=1024&measure). Watch time per token and the bound in [Perf](lab:view?name=perf). [Back to batch 1](lab:param?name=batch&value=1).\n\n## The program\n\nOne linear layer, $y = x W^\\top$, with $W \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times d}$ (here $d = 2048$, so $W$ is 16 MiB of fp32) and $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^{M \\times d}$. $M$ is the number of tokens processed together:\n\n- **Decoding:** $M$ = the number of sequences in flight. Each step makes one token per sequence, so $M$ is 1 for a single user.\n- **Prefill:** $M$ = the prompt length. All prompt tokens go through the layer at once.\n\n## The maths\n\n$$W_\\text{flop} = 2Md^2, \\qquad Q = 4\\,(Md + d^2 + Md) = 4d\\,(2M + d)\\ \\text{bytes}$$\n\nThe $d^2$ term is the weights. It is paid once per call, whatever $M$ is. So the intensity is\n\n$$I(M) \\;=\\; \\frac{2Md^2}{4d(2M+d)} \\;=\\; \\frac{Md}{2(2M + d)} \\;\\approx\\; \\frac{M}{2} \\quad (M \\ll d)$$\n\n**Each token you add to the batch adds about half a flop per byte.** At $M = 1$ the layer does 0.5 flop/byte, about 1/100 of an RTX 5090's ridge point (58.5). The weights stream from DRAM and the cores idle.\n\nThe layer becomes compute-bound when $I(M) = I^*$:\n\n$$M^* = \\frac{2 d I^*}{d - 4I^*}$$\n\nwhich gives $M^* \\approx 132$ on the RTX 5090 and $\\approx 42$ on the H100 (ridge 20).\n\nBelow $M^*$, the roofline bound $t \\ge t_\\text{launch} + Q/\\beta$ hardly changes with $M$, because $Q$ is dominated by $d^2$. **Up to $M^*$, extra tokens are nearly free.** That is the economics behind batched LLM serving, and behind speculative decoding, which turns several sequential decoding steps into one batched verification.\n\n## What you should see\n\nSimulated RTX 5090, $d = 2048$. The bound column is $2.5\\,\\mu s + \\max(Q/\\beta, W/\\pi)$.\n\n| batch $M$ | intensity | roofline bound | simulated | per token | bound by |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| 1 | 0.5 | 11.9 µs | 12.6 µs | 12.6 µs | dram |\n| 8 | 3.9 | 11.9 µs | 33.9 µs | 4.2 µs | smem |\n| 32 | 15.5 | 12.2 µs | 42.2 µs | 1.3 µs | smem |\n| 128 | 56.9 | 13.0 µs | 86.3 µs | 0.67 µs | smem |\n| 256 | 102 | 23.0 µs | 121.5 µs | 0.47 µs | smem |\n| 1024 | 256 | 84.5 µs | 359.4 µs | 0.35 µs | smem |\n\nPer-token cost falls by 36× from $M = 1$ to $M = 1024$. Two things in this table are worth a closer look.\n\n**Batch 1 hits the roofline.** The compiler picks a row schedule: a block per output row computes a dot product with a tree reduction. That streams $W$ at 1331 GB/s, 74% of peak. A matrix–vector product can't do better than streaming the weights once, so this is essentially optimal.\n\n**Batch 8 is 2.8× off the roofline.** In theory it should cost the same as batch 1 (same bytes), but this compiler has no good schedule for \"skinny\" matrices:\n\n- The row schedule re-reads $W$ once per row of $x$, so traffic grows by 8×.\n- The tiled schedule pads $M$ up to a 16-row tile, wasting half its work, and becomes limited by shared memory.\n\nProduction libraries ship special kernels for this shape: batched GEMV, and **split-K**, which splits the long $d$ dimension across blocks and adds the partial sums. Writing a split-K pass is a good exercise.\n\nAt large $M$ the layer is compute-heavy but shows \"smem\", not \"compute\": these FP32 SIMT kernels are limited by shared-memory bandwidth at about 24 TFLOP/s, 23% of peak. W-21 climbs that ladder. Real prefill uses tensor cores, which this compiler does not emit.\n\n## The system\n\n- The fork at the tile pass offers `MAP=row` (dot products with a block-wide tree reduction) and `MAP=tiled` with every legal tile shape. Open [Knobs](lab:view?name=knobs) at batch 1 and at batch 1024: the cost model's favourite flips from row to tiled as the intensity grows.\n- The check in [Code](lab:view?name=code) prints the intensity of the current shape.\n- The cost model's estimates, and the simulator, count bank conflicts in shared memory the same way the hardware does: per warp, the most distinct addresses that fall in one bank.\n\n> **Caveat** — Real decode kernels also read the KV cache (attention), quantize weights to 8 or 4 bits (which cuts $Q$ by 4–8×, so intensity goes up by the same factor), and fuse several layers. The intensity argument stays the same, with different constants.\n\n## Takeaways\n\n1. Matrix–vector products (decoding) are memory-bound by construction: about 0.5 flop/byte in fp32.\n2. Batching raises intensity linearly until the ridge point. Below it, extra batch is nearly free; above it, cost grows with the flops.\n3. \"Memory-bound\" tells you the target. At batch 1, the job is moving the weights at full bandwidth, or moving fewer of them (quantization).\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Derive $I(M)$ for fp16 weights with fp32 activations. What is $M^*$ on the RTX 5090 now?\n2. Switch to the [H100](lab:device?id=sim:h100&measure). At what batch does its bound column leave DRAM?\n3. Write the split-K rewrite as a Tensor IR pass: reshape the $d$ axis into $(S, d/S)$, reduce the inner axis, then the outer. Offer $S$ as a fork. Measure batch 8.\n\n## Further reading\n\n- [Modal GPU Glossary](https://modal.com/gpu-glossary): arithmetic intensity\n- Horace He, [Making Deep Learning Go Brrrr](https://horace.io/brrr_intro.html)\n- [CUTLASS documentation](https://docs.nvidia.com/cutlass/): split-K and stream-K GEMM\n"},{"id":"W-21","title":"The matmul ladder","group":"GPU optimization","about":"one 1024×1024 matmul, five schedules: naive, shared-memory tiles, 1-D and 2-D register tiles, and the cost model's pick. Each step fixes the previous step's bottleneck","tries":["Walk me through the ladder: at each step, what is the bottleneck and which metric shows it?","Why is the 8×8 register tile slower than 8×2 here?"],"text":"/** W-21 The matmul ladder\n *  group  GPU optimization\n *  about  one 1024×1024 matmul, five schedules: naive, shared-memory tiles, 1-D and 2-D register tiles, and the cost model's pick. Each step fixes the previous step's bottleneck\n *  try    Walk me through the ladder: at each step, what is the bottleneck and which metric shows it? | Why is the 8×8 register tile slower than 8×2 here?\n */\n// C = A·B, every element a dot product of length N: 2N³ flops over 12N² bytes of unique data, so a\n// matmul has plenty of reuse (N/6 flop per unique byte). The schedule decides how much of that reuse\n// reaches the hardware: every step below cuts the traffic of one level of the memory hierarchy.\n// The `step` slider pins the tile pass's choice through a custom decide policy. See the Notes.\nconst N_ = param.int(\"n\", { min: 256, max: 2048, default: 1024, scale: \"log\", help: \"C = A·B with A, B, C all n×n\" });\nconst STEP = param.enum(\"step\", [\"1 naive\", \"2 smem tiles\", \"3 1-D register tiles\", \"4 2-D register tiles\", \"5 8x8 tiles\", \"6 cost model\"], { default: \"1 naive\", help: \"which schedule the tile pass must take\" });\nconst LADDER = {\n  \"1 naive\":                 { MAP: \"thread\", TPB: 256, COARSEN: 1 },\n  \"2 smem tiles\":            { MAP: \"tiled\", BM: 32, BN: 32, BK: 32, TM: 1, TN: 1 },\n  \"3 1-D register tiles\":    { MAP: \"tiled\", BM: 64, BN: 64, BK: 8, TM: 8, TN: 1 },\n  \"4 2-D register tiles\":    { MAP: \"tiled\", BM: 128, BN: 128, BK: 8, TM: 8, TN: 2 },\n  \"5 8x8 tiles\":             { MAP: \"tiled\", BM: 128, BN: 128, BK: 8, TM: 8, TN: 8 },\n};\n\n// a decide policy: score 0 for the option the step names, Infinity for the others; \"6 cost model\" passes\nconst ladder = k.decide.cost(({ knobs, run }) => {\n  const want = LADDER[run.params.get(STEP)];\n  if (!want) return null;\n  return Object.entries(want).every(([key, v]) => knobs[key] === v) ? 0 : Infinity;\n}, { name: \"ladder\" }).for(\"schedule\");\nconst base = std.emmy.pipeline();\n\nproject(\"matmul-ladder\")\n  .pipeline(base.with({ decide: k.decide.chain(k.decide.pinned(), ladder, base._decide) }))\n  .params(N_, STEP)\n  .program(({ a, b }) => ({ c: N.matmul(a, b) }), ({ param }) => ({ a: k.tensor([param(N_), param(N_)]), b: k.tensor([param(N_), param(N_)]) }))\n  .check(\"the schedule this step asks for\", ({ run }) => {\n    const d = run.decisions[0], want = LADDER[run.params.get(STEP)];\n    if (!want) return { ok: true, message: `the cost model picked ${std.tile.canonRow(d.knobs)}` };\n    return Object.entries(want).every(([key, v]) => d.knobs[key] === v) ? { ok: true, message: std.tile.canonRow(d.knobs) } : `got ${std.tile.canonRow(d.knobs)} (illegal on this device?)`;\n  });\n","notes":"# The matmul ladder\n\nMatrix multiplication is the workload GPUs are built for and the classic lesson in GPU optimization. This project compiles one $1024 \\times 1024$ matmul five ways, plus whatever the cost model picks. Each step removes the bottleneck of the step before and reveals the next. The `step` parameter pins the schedule.\n\n> **Try it** — measure each step in turn and keep [Perf](lab:view?name=perf) open:\n> [1 naive](lab:param?name=step&value=1%20naive&measure) ·\n> [2 shared-memory tiles](lab:param?name=step&value=2%20smem%20tiles&measure) ·\n> [3 1-D register tiles](lab:param?name=step&value=3%201-D%20register%20tiles&measure) ·\n> [4 2-D register tiles](lab:param?name=step&value=4%202-D%20register%20tiles&measure) ·\n> [5 8×8 tiles](lab:param?name=step&value=5%208x8%20tiles&measure) ·\n> [6 the cost model's pick](lab:param?name=step&value=6%20cost%20model&measure)\n\n## The maths: plenty of reuse, if you can keep it\n\n$C = AB$ with $A, B, C \\in \\mathbb{R}^{N \\times N}$ costs\n\n$$W = 2N^3 \\text{ flop}, \\qquad Q_\\text{min} = 3 \\cdot 4N^2 \\text{ bytes}, \\qquad I_\\text{max} = \\frac{2N^3}{12N^2} = \\frac{N}{6}$$\n\nAt $N = 1024$ the best possible intensity is 171 flop/byte, about three times the RTX 5090's ridge point (58.5). In principle matmul is compute-bound. In practice every element of $A$ is used $N$ times, and **the schedule decides how many of those uses reach the chip from far away** (DRAM or L2) versus nearby (shared memory or a register). Each rung of the ladder moves reuse one level closer to the arithmetic units:\n\n| level | where a value lives | approximate bandwidth on an RTX 5090 |\n|---|---|---|\n| DRAM | off-chip GDDR7 | 1.8 TB/s |\n| L2 | on-chip, shared by all SMs | ~5 TB/s (the simulator assumes 3× DRAM) |\n| shared memory | per SM, 32 banks × 4 B per clock | 170 SMs × 128 B × 2.41 GHz ≈ 52 TB/s |\n| registers | per thread | operands of every instruction, every clock |\n\n## What you should see\n\nSimulated RTX 5090, $N = 1024$ ($W$ = 2.15 GFLOP):\n\n| step | schedule | time | TFLOP/s | bound | L2 traffic | smem wavefronts | occupancy |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 naive | thread per output | 1087 µs | 2.0 | l2 | 5.0 GiB | — | 1.00 |\n| 2 smem tiles | 32×32×32 tiles | 199 µs | 10.8 | smem | 260 MiB | 69.2 M | 0.67 |\n| 3 1-D register tiles | 64×64, 8×1 per thread | 128 µs | 16.7 | smem | 132 MiB | 38.8 M | 1.00 |\n| 4 2-D register tiles | 128×128, 8×2 per thread | 169 µs | 12.7 | smem | 72 MiB | 25.7 M | 0.67 |\n| 5 8×8 tiles | 128×128, 8×8 per thread | 336 µs | 6.4 | smem | 96 MiB | 25.7 M | 0.33 (regs) |\n| 6 cost model | 16×32, 2×4 per thread, 64 threads | **99 µs** | 21.7 | smem | 400 MiB | 36.7 M | 1.00 (0.54) |\n\nAt $N = 2048$ ([try it](lab:param?name=n&value=2048)), steps 1–5 take 8141, 1405, 925, **669** and 891 µs. Step 4 is slower than step 3 at $N = 1024$ but faster at $N = 2048$; the next sections explain why. At both sizes the cost model's own pick beats every rung of the ladder: the last section explains that too.\n\n### Step 1 → 2: from L2 to shared memory\n\nNaive: thread $(i, j)$ loops over $k$, loading $A_{ik}$ and $B_{kj}$ from global memory.\n\n- The 32 threads of a warp share $i$, so the $A_{ik}$ load is one broadcast sector.\n- They have consecutive $j$, so $B_{kj..k(j+31)}$ is 128 contiguous bytes: 4 sectors.\n\nThat is 5 sectors for 2 loads, the **2.5 sectors per request** in [Perf](lab:view?name=perf), and\n\n$$Q_\\text{L2} = \\underbrace{\\tfrac{N^2}{32}}_{\\text{warps}} \\cdot N \\cdot 5 \\cdot 32\\,\\text{B} = 5.4\\text{ GB} = 5.0\\text{ GiB}$$\n\nAt the simulated L2 bandwidth, that is about 1 ms. Nothing is reused on-chip.\n\nTiling: a block computes a $B_M \\times B_N$ tile of $C$. For each slab of $B_K$ along $k$, the block cooperatively copies an $A$ tile and a $B$ tile into shared memory, synchronizes, then computes from there. Each global value now serves a whole tile, so $A$ is fetched $N/B_N$ times and $B$ is fetched $N/B_M$ times:\n\n$$Q_\\text{L2} = 4N^2\\left(\\frac{N}{B_N} + \\frac{N}{B_M}\\right) = 4 \\cdot 1024^2 \\cdot (32 + 32) = 268\\text{ MB}$$\n\nThat is 20× less. The bottleneck moves to **shared-memory bandwidth**. Every FMA still reads 2 operands from shared memory, and a warp-wide read is one \"wavefront\" per clock per SM: $N^3 \\cdot 2 / 32 \\approx 67$ M wavefronts, plus the tile stores, makes 69 M.\n\n### Step 2 → 3: from shared memory to registers (1-D)\n\nGive each thread $T_M = 8$ outputs in a column. For each $k$ it loads 8 values of $A$ and **one** value of $B$, reusing that $B$ value from a register 8 times. Per warp and per $k$:\n\n- 8 broadcast $A$ reads plus 1 $B$ read = 9 wavefronts for $32 \\times 8 = 256$ FMAs, 0.035 wavefronts per FMA;\n- step 2 needed 2 wavefronts for 32 FMAs, 0.0625 per FMA.\n\nThe ratio, 0.56, is exactly the drop in the smem column: 69.2 M → 38.8 M.\n\n### Step 3 → 4: 2-D register tiles — and wave quantization\n\nWith an $8 \\times 2$ register tile, each $k$ step is 8 $A$ + 2 $B$ loads for 16 FMAs, and shared-memory traffic drops again (25.7 M). **But the time goes up.** Tiles of $128 \\times 128$ cut a $1024 \\times 1024$ matrix into only\n\n$$\\frac{1024}{128} \\cdot \\frac{1024}{128} = 64 \\text{ blocks}$$\n\non a GPU with 170 SMs: **106 SMs sit idle**. That is wave quantization — the grid doesn't fill the machine. At $N = 2048$ there are 256 blocks and step 4 beats step 3.\n\n$$\\text{waves} = \\left\\lceil \\frac{\\text{blocks}}{\\text{SMs} \\times \\text{blocks per SM}} \\right\\rceil, \\qquad \\text{SM use in the last wave} = \\frac{\\text{blocks left}}{\\text{SMs}}$$\n\n### Step 4 → 5: more reuse on paper, slower in practice\n\nAn $8 \\times 8$ register tile has the best reuse ratio on paper, (8 + 8) loads per 64 FMAs. It loses for two reasons.\n\n- **Bank conflicts.** Thread $t$ reads $B$ at shared address $k \\cdot B_N + 8t$. Shared memory has 32 banks, 4 bytes wide, so addresses $0, 8, 16, \\dots$ fall in only 4 banks: a **4-way bank conflict**, 4 wavefronts per read. The bank-conflict column in Perf shows 1.88 extra wavefronts per request.\n- **Registers.** 64 accumulators plus operands is about 104 registers per thread. $65{,}536 / (104 \\cdot 256)$ = 2 blocks per SM: 33% occupancy, limited by registers.\n\nProduction kernels fix both with vectorized `float4` loads, swizzled or transposed shared-memory layouts, and *warp tiling*. Those transformations are the next rungs this compiler does not have yet.\n\n### Step 6: the cost model's pick wins by balance, not reuse\n\nThe cost model searches all 645 legal tile shapes and picks **16×32 tiles with a 2×4 register tile per thread**: 64 threads per block, 2048 blocks. Its shared-memory traffic is about the same as step 3's (36.7 M vs 38.8 M wavefronts), yet it runs in 99 µs instead of 128. The difference is **load balance**.\n\n- Step 3 launches 256 blocks on 170 SMs. 86 SMs get two blocks and 84 get one, so the kernel lasts as long as two blocks while the average SM does $256/170 = 1.51$ blocks' work. A quarter of the machine idles for the second half.\n- Step 6 launches 2048 small blocks. The busiest SM runs $\\lceil 2048/170 \\rceil = 13$, against an average of 12.05: 7% idle.\n\n$$t \\approx \\frac{\\text{wavefronts}}{\\text{SMs} \\cdot f} \\cdot \\frac{\\text{blocks on the busiest SM}}{\\text{average blocks per SM}} + t_\\text{launch}: \\quad \\frac{38.8\\text{M}}{410\\text{G/s}} \\cdot \\frac{2}{1.51} + 2.5 = 128\\ \\mu s, \\qquad \\frac{36.7\\text{M}}{410\\text{G/s}} \\cdot \\frac{13}{12.05} + 2.5 = 99\\ \\mu s$$\n\nAt $N = 2048$ the pick changes to 32×64 tiles with 8×4 per thread (566 µs, 30.4 TFLOP/s, vs 669 for step 4), and on the A100 at $N = 1024$ it is again 16×32 with 2×4 (244 µs, vs 287 for step 4). Hand-written ladders choose tiles for reuse; a search over tile shapes also sees the grid. Many small blocks are the cheap fix for tail effects; production libraries use *split-K* or *stream-K* to the same end.\n\n## Where this leaves us\n\nThe best schedule here reaches 21.7 TFLOP/s, 21% of the RTX 5090's 104.8 TFLOP/s FP32 peak, and it is limited by shared memory. Simon Boehm's post climbs the same ladder further: vectorized shared-memory loads, warp tiling, autotuned tile shapes. He ends close to cuBLAS. Beyond that, tensor cores change the arithmetic itself.\n\nSame ladder, [A100](lab:device?id=sim:a100&measure), $N = 1024$: 883, 482, 325, **287**, 653 µs. With 108 SMs, the 64-block grid of step 4 hurts less than on the 5090.\n\n## The system\n\n- The `step` parameter is read by a custom **decide policy**, `ladder`. It scores the option that matches the step at 0 and every other option at Infinity, and sits in front of the standard chain (pins → evidence → cost model). Step 6 returns `null` for every option, so the standard chain decides. See the script, and `by: ladder` in [Knobs](lab:view?name=knobs).\n- The kernel bodies are in [Kernels](lab:view?name=kernels): the `Tile` tab shows the schedule, `Kernel IR` the shared-memory staging and register tile, `CUDA` the code.\n- The simulator counts every shared-memory request per warp exactly as the hardware does: the most distinct addresses in any one bank. The cost model computes the same count analytically from the same addresses.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Compute $Q_\\text{L2}$ for step 3 from the formula and compare with the 132 MiB measured.\n2. Why is the naive kernel's $A$ load a broadcast? Rewrite the program as $C = (B^\\top A^\\top)^\\top$ and see whether the naive version gets worse.\n3. Add a padding knob to the tiled materializer (`kernel.js`, `matTiled`): store $B$ with a stride of $B_N + 1$. Does step 5's conflict disappear?\n4. Tune with [SP-MCTS](lab:tune?strategy=spmcts&budget=32) and compare its best schedule with the cost model's.\n\n## Further reading\n\n- Simon Boehm, [How to Optimize a CUDA Matmul Kernel for cuBLAS-like Performance](https://siboehm.com/articles/22/CUDA-MMM)\n- Hwu, Kirk, El Hajj, *Programming Massively Parallel Processors*, the chapters on tiling and memory\n- [CUTLASS](https://docs.nvidia.com/cutlass/) and [CuTe](https://docs.nvidia.com/cutlass/latest/media/docs/cpp/cute/index.html): how production kernels lay out tiles\n- [MIT 6.S894, Accelerated Computing](https://accelerated-computing.academy/fall25/): the matmul labs\n"},{"id":"W-22","title":"Variance three ways: speed vs. numerics","group":"GPU optimization","about":"the same statistic computed two-pass, one-pass (E[x²] − E[x]²) and shifted one-pass; slide the data's offset and watch fp32 destroy one of them","tries":["At what offset does the one-pass formula fail, and why does it fail on the GPU but not in the reference?","Is the two-pass version actually slower here?"],"text":"/** W-22 Variance three ways: speed vs. numerics\n *  group  GPU optimization\n *  about  the same statistic computed two-pass, one-pass (E[x²] − E[x]²) and shifted one-pass; slide the data's offset and watch fp32 destroy one of them\n *  try    At what offset does the one-pass formula fail, and why does it fail on the GPU but not in the reference? | Is the two-pass version actually slower here?\n */\n// Var(x) = E[(x − μ)²] = E[x²] − μ². Mathematically identical, numerically not: when the mean is large\n// next to the spread, E[x²] and μ² are two huge, nearly equal numbers, and their difference in fp32 is\n// mostly rounding error (catastrophic cancellation). LayerNorm needs this statistic for every row.\n// The simulator computes in fp32 like the GPU; the reference accumulates in fp64. See the Notes.\nconst offset = param.enum(\"offset\", [0, 1, 10, 100, 1000, 10000], { default: 0, help: \"added to every input: the mean moves, the spread stays\" });\nconst rows = param.int(\"rows\", { min: 16, max: 8192, default: 1024, scale: \"log\" });\nconst dim = param.int(\"dim\", { min: 64, max: 8192, default: 2048, scale: \"log\" });\n\nproject(\"variance\")\n  .params(offset, rows, dim)\n  .program(({ x }, { param }) => {\n    const D = param(dim);\n    const xs = N.add(x, param(offset));                        // inputs are uniform on [−1, 1): σ² = 1/3\n    const mean = (t) => N.mul(N.sum(t, -1), 1 / D);            // each method computes its own means, so each\n    // output is one self-contained kernel (and each kernel's check sees the real arithmetic)\n    const two_pass = mean(N.square(N.sub(xs, mean(xs))));                  // E[(x − μ)²]\n    const one_pass = N.sub(mean(N.square(xs)), N.square(mean(xs)));        // E[x²] − μ²\n    const xk = N.sub(xs, N.slice(xs, -1, 0, 1));                           // shift by the row's first value K\n    const shifted = N.sub(mean(N.square(xk)), N.square(mean(xk)));         // E[(x−K)²] − E[x−K]²\n    return { two_pass, one_pass, shifted };\n  }, ({ param }) => ({ x: k.tensor([param(rows), param(dim)]) }))\n  .check(\"the reference variance is 1/3 (inputs are uniform on [−1, 1))\", ({ outputs }) => {\n    const v = outputs.two_pass.data, m = v.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / v.length;\n    return Math.abs(m - 1 / 3) < 0.01 || `mean of the row variances ${m}`;\n  });\n","notes":"# Variance three ways: speed vs. numerics\n\nLayerNorm, BatchNorm and RMSNorm all compute a variance per row, on every layer, for every token. A tempting \"optimization\" computes it in one pass over the data instead of two. On a GPU in fp32, that formula can return garbage. This project computes the same statistic three ways side by side, so you can watch it happen.\n\n> **Try it** — [measure at offset 0](lab:measure): all three kernels pass. Then [offset 100](lab:param?name=offset&value=100&measure), [offset 1000](lab:param?name=offset&value=1000&measure) and [offset 10000](lab:param?name=offset&value=10000&measure), and read the checks in [Perf](lab:view?name=perf). [Reset to 0](lab:param?name=offset&value=0).\n\n## The program\n\nInputs $x_{ij}$ are uniform on $[-1, 1)$, so the true variance of every row is $\\sigma^2 = 1/3$. The program adds an offset $c$ to every input — the mean moves, the spread stays — and returns three per-row variances:\n\n| output | formula | sweeps over the row |\n|---|---|---|\n| `two_pass` | $\\mu = \\frac1D\\sum_j x_j$, then $\\frac1D\\sum_j (x_j - \\mu)^2$ | 2 |\n| `one_pass` | $\\frac1D\\sum_j x_j^2 \\;-\\; \\mu^2$ | 1 |\n| `shifted` | $K = x_0$, then $\\frac1D\\sum_j (x_j - K)^2 - \\big(\\frac1D\\sum_j (x_j-K)\\big)^2$ | 1 |\n\nAll three are algebraically identical:\n\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(x) = \\mathbb{E}\\big[(x-\\mu)^2\\big] = \\mathbb{E}[x^2] - \\mu^2 = \\mathbb{E}\\big[(x-K)^2\\big] - \\big(\\mathbb{E}[x-K]\\big)^2 \\quad\\text{for any } K$$\n\nEach method computes its own means, so each output is one self-contained kernel: `k_two_pass`, `k_one_pass` and `k_shifted` in [Kernels](lab:view?name=kernels).\n\n## The maths: catastrophic cancellation\n\nfp32 stores 24 significant bits. Every operation's result is exact up to a relative error of the **unit roundoff** $u = 2^{-24} \\approx 5.96 \\times 10^{-8}$.\n\nThe one-pass formula subtracts two large, nearly equal numbers: $\\mathbb{E}[x^2] \\approx c^2 + \\sigma^2$ and $\\mu^2 \\approx c^2$. Each carries an absolute rounding error of about $u \\cdot c^2$, and the subtraction keeps the errors while cancelling the true values. The relative error of the result is roughly\n\n$$\\frac{|\\widehat{\\operatorname{Var}} - \\operatorname{Var}|}{\\operatorname{Var}} \\;\\approx\\; u \\cdot \\frac{c^2 + \\sigma^2}{\\sigma^2} \\cdot g(D)$$\n\nwhere $g(D)$ is a modest growth factor from accumulating $D$ terms (about $\\log_2 D$ for a tree reduction). The ratio $c^2/\\sigma^2$ is the **condition number** of this way of computing the variance. With $\\sigma^2 = 1/3$:\n\n| offset $c$ | $c^2/\\sigma^2$ | predicted relative error | simulated max error of `one_pass` (absolute) |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | 3 | $2 \\times 10^{-7}$ | $9 \\times 10^{-8}$ |\n| 10 | 300 | $2 \\times 10^{-5}$ | $8 \\times 10^{-6}$ |\n| 100 | $3 \\times 10^4$ | $2 \\times 10^{-3}$ | $1.4 \\times 10^{-3}$ — **fails** |\n| 1000 | $3 \\times 10^6$ | 0.2 | 0.039 — fails |\n| 10000 | $3 \\times 10^8$ | 20 | 8.3 — the \"variance\" is meaningless |\n\nThe two-pass formula subtracts the mean *before* squaring. Its terms $(x_j - \\mu)^2$ are small, nothing large cancels, and its error stays near $u$ for every offset (simulated $\\le 2 \\times 10^{-5}$ even at $c = 10^4$). The shifted formula gets the same protection with one pass: subtracting any value $K$ close to the data makes $x - K$ small, and $K = x_0$ is close.\n\n## What you should see\n\nAll three kernels take **7.9 µs**, with or without numerical trouble. The input is 8 MiB per call and every version is memory-bound. The \"extra\" sweep of the two-pass method re-reads a row that one thread block touched microseconds earlier, so it hits cache.\n\nThe one-pass formula buys nothing in speed and costs everything in accuracy. That is the lesson: **check whether an optimization changes the numerics, and measure whether it changes the speed.**\n\n## The system: how the failure is caught\n\n- The simulated GPU computes in **fp32**: every register is a `Float32Array` element, and every sum is a tree of fp32 adds, as on a real GPU. The reference (`compilePoint`, the Loop IR evaluated in JavaScript) accumulates in **fp64**. Each measurement compares the kernel's sampled outputs against that reference with relative tolerance $2 \\times 10^{-3}$, which is how `k_one_pass` fails while the others pass.\n- The end-to-end check in [Code](lab:view?name=code) fails too at large offsets. There the frontend reference, which rounds every op's result to fp32, disagrees with the Loop IR, which keeps intermediates in fp64 inside a kernel. Two implementations of an ill-conditioned formula disagree with each other — a symptom worth recognizing.\n- Per-kernel checks use synthetic inputs, uniform on $[-1, 1)$. They catch this bug only because the offset is added *inside* the kernel. If the offset came from upstream data, the test inputs would miss it. **Test with realistic magnitudes.**\n\n## Welford, and why this compiler can't express it (yet)\n\nWelford's algorithm updates a running mean and sum of squared deviations one element at a time:\n\n$$\\mu_k = \\mu_{k-1} + \\frac{x_k - \\mu_{k-1}}{k}, \\qquad M_k = M_{k-1} + (x_k - \\mu_{k-1})(x_k - \\mu_k)$$\n\nTwo partial results $(n_a, \\mu_a, M_a)$ and $(n_b, \\mu_b, M_b)$ merge exactly (Chan, Golub and LeVeque), with $\\delta = \\mu_b - \\mu_a$ and $n = n_a + n_b$:\n\n$$\\mu = \\mu_a + \\delta\\,\\frac{n_b}{n}, \\qquad M = M_a + M_b + \\delta^2\\,\\frac{n_a n_b}{n}$$\n\nThe merge is associative, so it fits a GPU tree reduction: one pass, numerically stable. This compiler's reductions only combine with `add`, `max`, `min` and `mul`. Adding a reducer over a *tuple* $(n, \\mu, M)$ is the same extension online softmax and FlashAttention need.\n\n## Takeaways\n\n1. Algebraic identities are not numerical identities. $\\mathbb{E}[x^2] - \\mu^2$ loses about $\\log_2(c^2/\\sigma^2)$ bits.\n2. On a GPU, a second sweep over a row in cache or shared memory is cheap. Don't trade accuracy for it without measuring.\n3. Kernel tests need inputs with realistic magnitudes and offsets, not just values on $[-1, 1)$.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. At which offset does `one_pass` lose all significant digits? Predict with $\\log_2(c^2/\\sigma^2) \\ge 24$, then check.\n2. Change `K` to the row's maximum instead of its first element. Is `shifted` still stable? Why does any sample of the row work?\n3. Add a pairwise (tree) summation variant of `one_pass` in fp32 by hand. Does it help? (Hint: the cancellation is in the final subtraction, not the sum.)\n\n## Further reading\n\n- Higham, *Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms*, the chapters on summation and on computing the variance\n- Welford (1962), *Note on a method for calculating corrected sums of squares and products*; Chan, Golub, LeVeque (1983), *Algorithms for computing the sample variance*\n- Goldberg, *What Every Computer Scientist Should Know About Floating-Point Arithmetic*\n"},{"id":"W-23","title":"Occupancy and latency hiding","group":"GPU optimization","about":"softmax over long rows: threads per block and shared-memory staging decide how many warps an SM can keep in flight, and that decides the bandwidth you get","tries":["Pin TPB=32 and explain the occupancy number and its limiter","Why can staging the row in shared memory make this slower?"],"text":"/** W-23 Occupancy and latency hiding\n *  group  GPU optimization\n *  about  softmax over long rows: threads per block and shared-memory staging decide how many warps an SM can keep in flight, and that decides the bandwidth you get\n *  try    Pin TPB=32 and explain the occupancy number and its limiter | Why can staging the row in shared memory make this slower?\n */\n// A GPU hides memory latency by switching between warps: while one waits ~0.5–1 µs for DRAM, others run.\n// How many warps an SM holds at once (occupancy) is capped by four budgets: threads, blocks, registers\n// and shared memory per SM. This kernel reads each row three times (max, sum, normalize); staging the\n// row in shared memory turns two of those reads into on-chip reads, but a 32 KiB row per block also\n// eats the SM's shared memory and with it the occupancy. Pin TPB and STAGE from the Notes and measure.\nconst rows = param.int(\"rows\", { min: 64, max: 8192, default: 2048, scale: \"log\" });\nconst dim = param.int(\"dim\", { min: 256, max: 8192, default: 8192, scale: \"log\" });\n\nproject(\"occupancy-softmax\")\n  .params(rows, dim)\n  .program(({ x }) => ({ y: N.softmax(x, -1) }), ({ param }) => ({ x: k.tensor([param(rows), param(dim)]) }))\n  .check(\"occupancy of the compiled kernel\", ({ run }) => {\n    const d = run.decisions[0];\n    return { ok: true, message: `schedule ${std.tile.canonRow(d.knobs)} (picked by ${d.by}); measure to see occupancy and its limiter in Perf` };\n  });\n","notes":"# Occupancy and latency hiding\n\nA DRAM read takes several hundred nanoseconds. A GPU doesn't wait for it: each SM keeps many warps resident and switches to another one whenever a warp stalls. **Occupancy** — how many warps an SM can hold at once — sets how much latency it can hide. This project is a softmax over long rows (2048 × 8192). Two schedule knobs change its occupancy over a 16× range.\n\n> **Try it** — pin a schedule, measure, and read *occupancy* and *limiter* in [Perf](lab:view?name=perf):\n> [32 threads](lab:pin?MAP=row&TPB=32&STAGE=none&measure) ·\n> [64 threads](lab:pin?MAP=row&TPB=64&STAGE=none&measure) ·\n> [1024 threads](lab:pin?MAP=row&TPB=1024&STAGE=none&measure) ·\n> [32 threads + row staged in shared memory](lab:pin?MAP=row&TPB=32&STAGE=reused&measure) ·\n> [256 + staged](lab:pin?MAP=row&TPB=256&STAGE=reused&measure) ·\n> [1024 + staged](lab:pin?MAP=row&TPB=1024&STAGE=reused&measure) ·\n> [unpin](lab:unpin)\n\n## The maths: four budgets per SM\n\nA block can only run on an SM that has room for **all** of its threads, registers and shared memory. The number of blocks resident per SM is the tightest of four limits:\n\n$$b \\;=\\; \\min\\left( b_\\text{max},\\; \\left\\lfloor \\frac{T_\\text{SM}}{T} \\right\\rfloor,\\; \\left\\lfloor \\frac{R_\\text{SM}}{r \\cdot T} \\right\\rfloor,\\; \\left\\lfloor \\frac{S_\\text{SM}}{s} \\right\\rfloor \\right), \\qquad \\text{occupancy} = \\frac{b \\cdot \\lceil T/32 \\rceil}{T_\\text{SM}/32}$$\n\nHere $T$ is threads per block, $r$ registers per thread and $s$ shared memory per block. For the simulated RTX 5090: $b_\\text{max} = 24$ blocks, $T_\\text{SM} = 1536$ threads (48 warps), $R_\\text{SM} = 65{,}536$ registers, $S_\\text{SM} = 128$ KiB.\n\n### Worked examples\n\n| schedule | threads | blocks | registers | shared mem | → blocks/SM | limiter | occupancy |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|---:|\n| TPB=32 | 1536/32 = 48 | 24 | 73 | 1024 | **24** | blocks | 24 warps / 48 = 0.50 |\n| TPB=64 | 24 | 24 | 35 | 512 | 24 | blocks/threads | 48/48 = 1.00 |\n| TPB=1024 | **1** | 24 | 1 | 32 | 1 | threads | 32/48 = 0.67 |\n| TPB=32, row staged (32.9 KiB) | 48 | 24 | 73 | **3** | 3 | smem | 3/48 = 0.06 |\n| TPB=1024, row staged (36.9 KiB) | **1** | 24 | 1 | 3 | 1 | threads | 0.67 |\n\nThat is the *theoretical* occupancy. The simulator also reports *achieved* occupancy, which depends on the grid. There are 2048 rows, so 2048 blocks over 170 SMs: about 12 blocks per SM in all. With TPB=32 they all fit in one wave, so each SM holds 13 one-warp blocks at most: 13/48 = 0.27, not 0.50.\n\n## The maths: Little's law\n\nHow many warps does it take to keep DRAM busy? Bytes in flight must equal bandwidth × latency:\n\n$$\\text{bytes in flight} = \\beta \\times L \\approx 1792\\ \\text{GB/s} \\times 600\\ \\text{ns} \\approx 1.08\\ \\text{MB}$$\n\nSpread over 170 SMs, that is about 6.3 KB per SM. One warp-wide 4-byte load moves 128 bytes, so an SM needs about **49 loads in flight**: one from each of 48 warps at full occupancy, or 4 independent loads each from 12 warps. More warps and more independent work per thread (instruction-level parallelism) are interchangeable. Thread coarsening and vector loads buy latency hiding without occupancy.\n\nThe latency $L$ here is an assumed round figure: real DRAM latency under load is roughly 400–800 ns. The simulator encodes the idea with a crude curve: the fraction of peak bandwidth reachable is $\\min(1, 0.2 + 1.6 \\cdot \\text{occupancy})$. Full bandwidth needs about 50% occupancy; at 6% you get 30%.\n\n## What you should see\n\nSimulated RTX 5090. Each row of 8192 floats is read three times: max, sum, normalize.\n\n| TPB | staged | occupancy (achieved) | limiter | DRAM traffic | time |\n|---:|---|---:|---|---:|---:|\n| 32 | no | 0.50 (0.27) | blocks | 154 MiB | 155.6 µs |\n| 64 | no | 1.00 (0.54) | blocks | 154 MiB | 99.5 µs |\n| 1024 | no | 0.67 (0.67) | threads | 154 MiB | 99.5 µs |\n| 32 | yes | 0.06 (0.05) | **smem** | 128 MiB | 277.8 µs |\n| 256 | yes | 0.50 (0.43) | smem | 128 MiB | 90.4 µs |\n| 1024 | yes | 0.67 (0.67) | threads | 128 MiB | **83.3 µs** |\n\nThree lessons in one table:\n\n- **Staging is a trade.** Keeping the row in shared memory removes two of its three DRAM reads (154 → 128 MiB, the minimum: 64 MiB in, 64 MiB out). But a 32 KiB row per block fits only 3 blocks in an SM's 128 KiB.\n- **Small staged blocks starve the SM.** With 32 threads per staged block, 3 blocks are only 3 warps: occupancy 6%, bandwidth collapses, and staging makes things **3× slower**.\n- **Big staged blocks get both.** With 1024 threads, one block per SM is already 32 warps (67%) — enough to hide latency — and it reads the row from DRAM once. The best schedule.\n\n## The system\n\n- The cost model computes occupancy per option from the launch configuration, the shared memory the staging needs, and a register estimate; [Knobs](lab:view?name=knobs) ranks every option.\n- The timing model splits the grid into **waves** of `SMs × blocks per SM`. A partial last wave runs with fewer resident warps per SM, and so at lower achieved occupancy. That is how the simulator charges for grids that don't fill the GPU.\n- Registers per thread come from a simple estimate, not a register allocator. Run the generated CUDA through `ptxas -v` (the Devices view explains the runner) to get the real count.\n\n> **Caveat** — Real GPUs also have L1 caches that can absorb the re-reads of an unstaged row. They let you trade L1 against shared memory (the carveout), and their schedulers hide latency differently by architecture. Occupancy is necessary for bandwidth, not sufficient. The [occupancy calculator in Nsight Compute](https://docs.nvidia.com/nsight-compute/) computes the same four limits on real hardware.\n\n## Takeaways\n\n1. Occupancy = resident warps / maximum warps. It is limited by threads, blocks, registers or shared memory — whichever runs out first.\n2. Memory-bound kernels need enough bytes in flight: high occupancy, or independent loads per thread.\n3. Shared-memory staging saves DRAM traffic but spends occupancy. Size blocks so that both work.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Compute the occupancy of TPB=128 with the row staged. Predict its time from the table, then [measure it](lab:pin?MAP=row&TPB=128&STAGE=reused&measure).\n2. Halve the row: [dim = 4096](lab:param?name=dim&value=4096). Staged blocks now take 16 KiB. How many fit per SM, and does TPB=32 staged recover?\n3. Switch to the [H100](lab:device?id=sim:h100&measure) (228 KiB shared memory per SM, 2048 threads). Which limiter changes?\n\n## Further reading\n\n- *CUDA C++ Best Practices Guide*, [the occupancy section](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-c-best-practices-guide/)\n- Vasily Volkov, *Better Performance at Lower Occupancy* (GTC 2010), on ILP versus occupancy\n- Hwu, Kirk, El Hajj, *Programming Massively Parallel Processors*, the chapter on performance considerations\n"},{"id":"W-24","title":"One program, five GPUs","group":"GPU optimization","about":"a GPT-2-sized MLP block compiled and measured for five GPUs: the ridge point, the SM count and the shared memory change which schedule wins and which resource binds","tries":["Measure on every simulated GPU and explain why the Orin is not just a slower 5090","Which kernel changes schedule between the A100 and the RTX 5090, and why?"],"text":"/** W-24 One program, five GPUs\n *  group  GPU optimization\n *  about  a GPT-2-sized MLP block compiled and measured for five GPUs: the ridge point, the SM count and the shared memory change which schedule wins and which resource binds\n *  try    Measure on every simulated GPU and explain why the Orin is not just a slower 5090 | Which kernel changes schedule between the A100 and the RTX 5090, and why?\n */\n// x → RMSNorm → Linear(d → 4d) → GELU → Linear(4d → d) → + x, the feed-forward half of a transformer\n// layer at GPT-2-small width. Switch the device (header pill, or the links in the Notes) and the\n// whole program recompiles for that GPU's limits and cost model; \"Every simulated GPU\" in Perf\n// measures it on all five. The evidence store keys every measurement by device.\nconst tokens = param.int(\"tokens\", { min: 16, max: 2048, default: 256, scale: \"log\" });\nconst d = param.int(\"d_model\", { min: 128, max: 2048, default: 768, scale: \"log\" });\n\nproject(\"mlp-block\")\n  .params(tokens, d)\n  .program(({ x }, { param }) => {\n    const D = param(d), H = 4 * D;\n    const h = N.rms_norm(x, std.weight(\"g\", [D], { init: \"ones\" }), { eps: 1e-6 });\n    const up = N.gelu(N.linear(h, std.weight(\"W_up\", [H, D])));\n    return { y: N.add(x, N.linear(up, std.weight(\"W_down\", [D, H]))) };\n  }, ({ param }) => ({ x: k.tensor([param(tokens), param(d)]) }))\n  .check(\"ridge point of this device\", ({ run }) => {\n    const t = run.ctx.target;\n    return { ok: true, message: `${t.name}: ${t.peakTflops} TFLOP/s ÷ ${t.bwGBs} GB/s = ${(t.peakTflops * 1000 / t.bwGBs).toFixed(1)} flop/byte; ${t.sms} SMs` };\n  });\n","notes":"# One program, five GPUs\n\n\"Which GPU is faster?\" has no single answer: it depends on which resource your kernels exhaust. This project compiles the feed-forward block of a transformer layer at GPT-2-small width for five simulated GPUs. On each one it measures which schedule wins and what binds.\n\n> **Try it** — [measure on every simulated GPU](lab:measure?all=sim) (it takes a few seconds), then compare in [Perf](lab:view?name=perf) under *Across devices*. To see one device's schedules, switch to it: [RTX 5090](lab:device?id=sim:rtx5090&measure) · [RTX 4090](lab:device?id=sim:rtx4090&measure) · [H100](lab:device?id=sim:h100&measure) · [A100](lab:device?id=sim:a100&measure) · [Orin](lab:device?id=sim:orin&measure). The [Knobs](lab:view?name=knobs) view shows the picks.\n\n## The program\n\n$$y = x + W_\\text{down}\\, \\operatorname{GELU}\\!\\big(W_\\text{up}\\, \\operatorname{RMSNorm}(x)\\big), \\qquad x \\in \\mathbb{R}^{256 \\times 768},\\; W_\\text{up} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{3072 \\times 768}$$\n\nIt compiles to three kernels:\n\n- RMSNorm: a row kernel.\n- Up-projection with GELU fused into its epilogue: a tiled matmul.\n- Down-projection with the residual add fused in: a tiled matmul.\n\nEach matmul is $2 \\cdot 256 \\cdot 768 \\cdot 3072 = 1.21$ GFLOP. Its 9.4 MB weight matrix dominates the bytes, so its intensity is about 90 flop/byte.\n\n## The devices\n\n| simulated GPU | SMs | clock | FP32 peak $\\pi$ | DRAM $\\beta$ | ridge $\\pi/\\beta$ | shared-memory rate (SMs × clock) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| RTX 5090 (Blackwell) | 170 | 2.41 GHz | 104.8 TF | 1792 GB/s | 58.5 | 410 G wavefronts/s |\n| RTX 4090 (Ada) | 128 | 2.52 | 82.6 | 1008 | 81.9 | 323 |\n| H100 SXM (Hopper) | 132 | 1.98 | 67 | 3350 | 20.0 | 261 |\n| A100 80GB (Ampere) | 108 | 1.41 | 19.5 | 2039 | 9.6 | 152 |\n| Orin Nano class | 8 | 0.625 | 1.28 | 68 | 18.8 | 5 |\n\nBy the roofline alone, the matmuls (about 90 flop/byte) are compute-bound everywhere. Then the H100 should be about 1.6× slower than the 5090 (67 vs 104.8 TFLOP/s) and the A100 about 5× slower.\n\n## What you should see\n\nSimulated, tokens = 256:\n\n| GPU | total | up-proj + GELU | down-proj + residual | bound |\n|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| RTX 5090 | 177 µs | 67.8 µs, 32×32 tiles, 2×2 per thread | 105.7 µs, 32×16 tiles | smem |\n| RTX 4090 | 176 µs | 70.2 µs, 64×32 tiles | 101.2 µs, 32×16 tiles | smem |\n| H100 | 249 µs | 88.6 µs, 64×32 tiles | 157.2 µs | smem |\n| A100 | 381 µs | 152.8 µs, 128×64, 4×2 per thread | 224.7 µs, 128×16 | smem |\n| Orin | 6261 µs | 3102 µs, 128×128, 8×2 per thread | 3131 µs, 128×64, 8×4 | smem |\n\n**What binds is shared memory, not the FP32 peak and not DRAM.** These kernels read their operands from shared memory at one warp-wide request per SM per clock. Their speed therefore tracks the last column, SMs × clock:\n\n- The 4090 nearly ties the 5090 despite 22% less FP32 peak.\n- The A100 is about 2.2× slower than the 5090, close to its 2.7× deficit in shared-memory rate and far from its 5.4× deficit in FP32 peak.\n- The H100 lands between the two.\n\n**The schedules differ per device.** The cost model sees each target's SM count, shared memory and limits:\n\n- **Bigger tiles on fewer SMs.** With 8 SMs the Orin can't use parallelism anyway, so it takes 128×128 tiles for maximum reuse. The 5090 needs at least 170 blocks in flight, so it takes small 32×32 tiles that give 768 blocks.\n- **Wave quantization.** A schedule with $G$ blocks on $S$ SMs holding $b$ blocks each runs in $\\lceil G / (S b) \\rceil$ waves. A grid that barely spills into an extra wave wastes most of that wave. The best tile size is not a constant; it is a function of the matrix *and* the GPU.\n\n**The RMSNorm kernel** (3–4 µs everywhere, 28 µs on the Orin) is launch-bound: 256 rows of 768 floats is 0.8 MB.\n\n## Why the H100 looks bad here\n\nThe H100 is built for **tensor cores**: about 989 TFLOP/s dense BF16, against 67 TFLOP/s FP32 on its CUDA cores. It also has HBM3 at 3.35 TB/s. This compiler emits FP32 SIMT code that uses neither, so the H100 competes on shared-memory rate, where a gaming card with more SMs and higher clocks wins. On real workloads the H100 runs this block in BF16 on tensor cores, far faster than the 5090's FP32 path. The lesson is not \"the 5090 beats the H100\". It is **\"performance depends on which units your code actually uses\"**.\n\n## The system\n\n- Switching device recompiles: the target's `maxThreadsPerBlock`, shared memory per block and SM count change which schedules are legal and how the cost model ranks them. Compare the [Tile IR](lab:ir?name=tile) between devices.\n- Every measurement is recorded as evidence keyed by (device, kernel identity, schedule), so tuning results never leak from one GPU to another. See [Tune](lab:view?name=tune).\n- *Across devices* in Perf shows each device's latest measurement of this project.\n\n## Takeaways\n\n1. Know which resource binds on *each* target: FP32 peak, tensor cores, DRAM, shared memory or launch overhead. Specs that don't bind don't matter.\n2. Schedules are not portable. Tile sizes that fill a 170-SM GPU waste an 8-SM one, and vice versa. Autotune per device, or let the cost model see the device.\n3. \"Compute-bound\" on paper can mean \"on-chip-bandwidth-bound\" in practice.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Raise [tokens to 1024](lab:param?name=tokens&value=1024) and measure on every GPU again. Which GPU gains the most, and why? (Think about blocks per SM and waves.)\n2. Compute the waves for the up-projection on the A100: $G = (256/128)(3072/64)$ blocks, 108 SMs. How busy is the last wave?\n3. Tune the program on the 5090, then switch to the 4090: does the 5090's evidence apply? Read the *by* column in Knobs.\n\n## Further reading\n\n- NVIDIA, *H100 Tensor Core GPU Architecture* (whitepaper, 2022)\n- [Modal GPU Glossary](https://modal.com/gpu-glossary): SMs, warps, tensor cores, occupancy\n- Ansor, [*Generating High-Performance Tensor Programs for Deep Learning*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.06762): why schedules are tuned per target\n"},{"id":"W-25","title":"The cost model vs. the measurements","group":"GPU optimization","about":"measure every schedule the compiler could pick for one kernel, then score the cost model: rank correlation, and how much its pick loses against the best measured","tries":["Sweep every option, then tell me where the model and the measurements disagree and why","Do the same on WebGPU and compare the correlation"],"text":"/** W-25 The cost model vs. the measurements\n *  group  GPU optimization\n *  about  measure every schedule the compiler could pick for one kernel, then score the cost model: rank correlation, and how much its pick loses against the best measured\n *  try    Sweep every option, then tell me where the model and the measurements disagree and why | Do the same on WebGPU and compare the correlation\n */\n// The greedy compile ranks a fork's options with an analytic cost model (std.tile.estimate) and takes the\n// best. Autotuning instead measures. This project's check scores the model against whatever has been\n// measured for this kernel on the current device: Spearman's rank correlation ρ between predicted and\n// measured times, and the regret (the measured time of the model's favourite ÷ the best measured time).\n// Run the sweep from the Notes, read the check in the Code view, then try a real GPU (WebGPU).\nconst rows = param.int(\"rows\", { min: 64, max: 8192, default: 2048, scale: \"log\" });\nconst dim = param.int(\"dim\", { min: 256, max: 8192, default: 8192, scale: \"log\" });\n\nconst spearman = (a, b) => {\n  const rank = (v) => { const o = v.map((x, i) => [x, i]).sort((p, q) => p[0] - q[0]); const r = []; o.forEach(([, i], j) => (r[i] = j)); return r; };\n  const ra = rank(a), rb = rank(b), n = a.length;\n  let d2 = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) d2 += (ra[i] - rb[i]) ** 2;\n  return 1 - (6 * d2) / (n * (n * n - 1));\n};\n\nproject(\"model-vs-measurement\")\n  .params(rows, dim)\n  .program(({ x }) => ({ y: N.softmax(x, -1) }), ({ param }) => ({ x: k.tensor([param(rows), param(dim)]) }))\n  .check(\"the cost model, scored against this device's measurements\", ({ run }) => {\n    const { store, deviceId, target } = run.ctx;\n    const d = run.decisions.find((x) => x.rule === \"schedule\");\n    const measured = store ? store.list({ device: deviceId, identity: d.key }).filter((r) => r.ok !== false) : [];\n    if (measured.length < 3) return { ok: true, message: `${measured.length} of ${d.size} options measured on ${deviceId}: sweep them all to score the model` };\n    const g = run.at(\"loop\"), n = g.nodes.get(d.node);\n    const an = std.tile.analyze(n.attrs.expr, n.attrs.shape, n.args.map((a) => g.nodes.get(a).type.shape));\n    const model = measured.map((r) => std.tile.estimate(an, r.knobs, target).us);\n    const us = measured.map((r) => r.us);\n    // the model's favourite, with the same tie-break the compile uses (lowest score, then knobs in canonical order)\n    const order = measured.map((r, i) => i).sort((a, b) => model[a] - model[b] || (k.knobs.canonical(measured[a].knobs) < k.knobs.canonical(measured[b].knobs) ? -1 : 1));\n    const iModel = order[0], best = Math.min(...us), iBest = us.indexOf(best);\n    return { ok: true, message: `${measured.length} of ${d.size} options measured on ${deviceId} · Spearman ρ = ${spearman(model, us).toFixed(3)} · the model's favourite ${measured[iModel].row} measured ${us[iModel].toFixed(1)} µs; the best measured ${measured[iBest].row} ${best.toFixed(1)} µs · regret ${(us[iModel] / best).toFixed(2)}×` };\n  });\n","notes":"# The cost model vs. the measurements\n\nA compiler that has to choose between schedules can **predict** (with a cost model) or **measure** (by autotuning). This project measures every schedule the compiler could pick for one kernel, then scores the cost model against the measurements. Neither the model nor the simulator is ground truth, and seeing where they disagree is the lesson.\n\n> **Try it**\n> 1. [Sweep every option](lab:tune?strategy=sweep-all&budget=32). It compiles and measures 27 programs, about a minute on the simulator.\n> 2. Read the score in the [Code view's checks](lab:view?name=code).\n> 3. Open [Knobs](lab:view?name=knobs), choose the `schedule` fork, and sort by *Model*, then by *Measured*.\n> 4. If your browser has WebGPU, [switch to it](lab:device?id=webgpu) and sweep again: the same score, on real hardware.\n\n## How a greedy compile decides\n\nAt every fork the decide policy ranks the options and takes the first legal one. The standard chain is:\n\n1. **pins** (yours),\n2. **evidence**: the fastest *measured* option on this device, once the cost model's favourite has been measured too (so that measuring one pinned schedule cannot lock it in after you unpin),\n3. **the cost model**, `std.tile.estimate`: analytic counts of flops, DRAM and L2 bytes, shared-memory wavefronts and warp instructions, turned into time by the roofline-plus-occupancy model,\n4. **first**.\n\nAfter the sweep the compile switches from `by: model` to `by: evidence`. Measurement overrides prediction.\n\n## The maths: scoring a cost model\n\nWhat matters is not the absolute error of the predictions but whether the model **ranks** options correctly, because the compiler only uses the ranking.\n\n**Spearman's rank correlation.** Rank the $n$ measured options by predicted time and by measured time. With $d_i$ the difference of option $i$'s two ranks,\n\n$$\\rho = 1 - \\frac{6 \\sum_i d_i^2}{n(n^2 - 1)}$$\n\n$\\rho = 1$ is a perfect ranking, $0$ no relation, $-1$ reversed.\n\n**Regret.** The cost of trusting the model:\n\n$$\\text{regret} = \\frac{t_\\text{measured}(\\text{model's favourite})}{\\min_i t_\\text{measured}(i)} \\;\\ge\\; 1$$\n\nA model can have a high $\\rho$ and still a bad regret if it mis-orders the top few, and only the top matters. Autotuning papers report both, often as \"fraction of optimal\".\n\n## What you should see\n\nSimulated RTX 5090, softmax over 2048 × 8192:\n\n- All 27 options: **ρ ≈ 0.99**. The 15 thread-per-element schedules are about 1000× slower than the row schedules (each thread re-reads its whole row to compute its own copy of the row max and sum), and both model and simulator agree on that. Easy cases inflate $\\rho$.\n- **Regret 1.19×.** The model's favourite, `MAP=row,TPB=1024` without staging, measures 99.5 µs. The best measured, `MAP=row,TPB=1024,STAGE=reused`, takes 83.3 µs.\n- Restrict attention to the 12 row schedules ([pin them one by one](lab:view?name=knobs), or sweep on a fresh device) and **ρ drops to about 0.92**.\n\n### Why they disagree\n\nThe softmax reads each row three times. Without staging, the second and third reads go to memory. Do they hit L2 or DRAM? The two sides use different heuristics:\n\n- **The cost model** counts the data a kernel touches, 64 MiB, which fits in the 96 MB L2. So it assumes almost every re-read hits L2, and staging buys nothing.\n- **The simulator** counts input *and* output, 128 MiB, which does not fit. So it sends 60% of the re-read traffic to DRAM, and staging saves 19%.\n\nNeither is the hardware. A real GPU's L2 behaviour depends on what else is in flight. Here a row is 32 KiB and about 170 rows are active at once, roughly 5 MB, so the re-reads of a row happen within microseconds and almost certainly hit L2. **The analytic model is probably closer to a real GPU here.** That's a hypothesis you can test: sweep on WebGPU and see whether staging still wins on your GPU.\n\n### Why the simulator mostly agrees with the model\n\nBoth convert counts to time with the same function (`timeModel`). The simulator executes the kernel and counts real events: every sector, every bank conflict, every divergent branch. The model computes the same counts analytically. Where the analytic counting is exact — tiled matmuls, where the model replicates the kernel's shared-memory address arithmetic — $\\rho$ is 1.0. Where it relies on heuristics — cache reuse, as here — they drift apart. What the simulator does *not* check is the time model itself. Only real hardware does that.\n\n## Autotuning: search strategies\n\n- **Sweep top 6.** Measure the model's six best options for each kernel: cheap, and good when the model's top 6 contain the true best.\n- **Sweep all.** Exhaustive. Feasible here (27 options) but not for a tiled matmul (645 options in W-08, and multiplied across kernels).\n- **SP-MCTS** (single-player Monte Carlo tree search) over the whole program's fork tree, following the CloudRift post. It keeps per-node *max* rewards (single-player games care about the best line, not the average), normalizes UCB by the best time so far, and stops after a patience budget. It spends measurements where the evidence says improvements are.\n\nProduction autotuners combine both ideas. TVM's AutoTVM and Ansor train a **learned** cost model (gradient-boosted trees) on measurements and use it to choose what to measure next. MetaSchedule makes the search space a probabilistic program. Every measurement here is stored as evidence, so tuning results persist across compiles for this device.\n\n## Takeaways\n\n1. Judge a cost model by ranking ($\\rho$) and by regret, not by absolute error.\n2. Cost models are most wrong about caches, contention and the like: anything not counted exactly. Know which parts of yours are heuristics.\n3. Measure the top candidates on the target. The evidence then outranks the model.\n\n## Exercises\n\n1. Make the cost model's L2 heuristic match the simulator's (in `std/tile.js`, `estimate`, the `dram = …` line). Re-run the sweep: does the regret go to 1.00×? Is that an improvement?\n2. Run [SP-MCTS with budget 12](lab:tune?strategy=spmcts&budget=12). How many measurements does it need to find the 83.3 µs schedule?\n3. Write a decide policy that tries the model's top 3 options and picks the best *measured* one (`k.decide.cost` with `run.ctx.store`).\n\n## Further reading\n\n- Chen et al., [AutoTVM: *Learning to Optimize Tensor Programs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.08166)\n- Zheng et al., [Ansor](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.06762) · Shao et al., [MetaSchedule](https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.13603)\n- Haj-Ali et al., [ProTuner: tuning programs with Monte Carlo tree search](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.13685)\n- Kaufman et al., [*A Learned Performance Model for Tensor Processing Units*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.01040)\n- CloudRift, [*Building a GPU compiler from scratch*, part 3](https://www.cloudrift.ai/blog/building-gpu-compiler-from-scratch-3): the SP-MCTS this project implements\n"}];
